var e={"psychiatry-depression":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Major depression** needs 5 of 9 symptoms for **at least 2 weeks**.`,`One of the five must be **depressed mood or anhedonia** (loss of interest or pleasure).`,`It must cause distress or loss of function and not be due to a substance or illness.`,`There must **never have been mania or hypomania**; if there has, it is bipolar disorder.`,`In India, depression affects **2.7% now**, with a treatment gap above 85%.`,`Most patients present to the GP with **bodily complaints**, not with sadness.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Severity decides treatment: **mild, moderate, severe, and severe with psychotic features**.`,`NICE 2022 simply splits **less severe (PHQ-9 under 16)** from more severe (16 or more).`,`Useful specifiers are melancholic, psychotic, anxious distress, **peripartum onset** and seasonal.`,`**Dysthymia** is low mood on most days for **2 years**, often mislabelled as personality.`,`**Grief** comes in waves with self-esteem preserved; depression brings pervasive worthlessness.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Genes (heritability **35-40%**) and childhood adversity set the stage; a recent loss triggers.`,`Chronic stress drives **CRH, ACTH and cortisol** that fail to switch off by feedback.`,`High cortisol lowers **BDNF**, so synapses are lost in hippocampus and prefrontal cortex.`,`Synaptic **serotonin, noradrenaline and dopamine** tone falls, giving low mood, fatigue and anhedonia.`,`The amygdala becomes overactive while the **dorsolateral prefrontal cortex** becomes underactive.`,`SSRIs raise serotonin in hours, but **5-HT1A autoreceptors desensitise only over 2-4 weeks**.`,`That desensitisation, plus rising BDNF, explains the **2-4 week antidepressant lag**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Screen with the **PHQ-2**; a score of **3 or more out of 6** needs the full PHQ-9.`,`Screen those with unexplained bodily symptoms, frequent visits, chronic disease or recent delivery.`,`Ask about **function** at work, home and self-care; impairment sets treatment intensity.`,`**Ask directly about suicide** in graded steps; asking does not plant the idea.`,`**Ask about past mania** before any antidepressant, and check with the family.`,`Ask about culprit drugs such as **steroids, isotretinoin, interferon and levetiracetam**, and about alcohol.`,`Check drugs that interact with SSRIs: **tramadol, triptans and linezolid**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for poor eye contact, tearfulness, stooped posture and **psychomotor retardation**.`,`Speech is low, monotonous and slow to start; mood is low and affect restricted.`,`**Hopelessness** predicts suicide better than sadness does.`,`**Nihilistic delusions** (my intestines have rotted) mean psychotic depression, called Cotard syndrome.`,`Effortless 'I don't know' answers suggest **depressive pseudodementia** rather than true dementia.`,`Look for **self-harm scars or ligature marks**, thyroid swelling, pallor and BMI.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Depression is a **clinical diagnosis**; tests only exclude mimics and set a baseline.`,`Every first episode: **CBC, fasting glucose or HbA1c, and TSH**.`,`**Serum sodium** at baseline and 2-4 weeks if over 65 or on diuretics starting an SSRI.`,`**ECG** before a TCA or high-dose citalopram or escitalopram, or in heart disease.`,`CT or MRI is not routine; reserve it for focal signs or late-onset cognitive decline.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Less severe: **behavioural activation**, exercise 3 times a week, guided self-help or CBT.`,`Give a drug when **PHQ-9 is 10 or more**, or mild illness persists beyond 4-6 weeks.`,`First choice is **escitalopram 10 mg** or **sertraline 50 mg** once daily, plus psychological support.`,`Warn that antidepressants are **not habit-forming** and take 2-4 weeks to work.`,`Warn of early nausea and jitteriness, which settle in 1-2 weeks.`,`Review at **1-2 weeks**, and weekly if under 25 or at suicide risk.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`An adequate trial is **4 weeks at a therapeutic dose** (6-8 weeks in the elderly).`,`Under **20-25% improvement at 3-4 weeks**: check adherence and alcohol, then raise the dose.`,`Still no response: **switch** to another SSRI, an SNRI or mirtazapine, or add mirtazapine.`,`After **two failed adequate trials**, refer for augmentation with aripiprazole, quetiapine or lithium.`,`**Psychotic depression** needs an antidepressant plus an antipsychotic, or ECT, under a specialist.`,`**Modified ECT** is for stupor, food refusal or high suicide risk; MHCA requires anaesthesia.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy and breastfeeding**: sertraline is preferred, and paroxetine is avoided.`,`**Adolescents**: fluoxetine 10 mg for 1 week, then 20 mg, with weekly review.`,`**Elderly**: start escitalopram 5 mg or sertraline 25 mg, check sodium, avoid TCAs.`,`**Heart disease**: sertraline is the safest choice.`,`**Postpartum**: screen with EPDS; **13 or more** is positive at the 6-week visit.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Repeat the **PHQ-9 at 4 weeks**: response is a **50% fall**, remission a score **under 5**.`,`Continue **6-9 months after remission**, or **2 years** after a second episode.`,`Taper over **at least 4 weeks**; fluoxetine 20 mg can usually stop abruptly.`,`At suicide risk, give **1-2 weeks' supply** at a time or let a relative hold tablets.`,`**MHCA section 115**: a person who attempts suicide shall not be tried or punished.`,`Refer for high suicide risk, psychosis, bipolar features, two failed trials or a child under 12.`,`Tele-MANAS **14416** gives free 24-hour support in regional languages.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antidepressants - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Escitalopram (SSRI)`,`10 mg oral OD, max 20 mg; 5 mg start elderly`,`6-9 months after remission`,`Nausea, jitteriness, hyponatraemia; max 10 mg over 65 (QT)`],[`Sertraline (SSRI)`,`50 mg oral OD, up to 200 mg`,`6-9 months after remission`,`Loose stools; choice in heart disease, pregnancy, breastfeeding`],[`Fluoxetine (SSRI)`,`20 mg oral OD, up to 60 mg`,`6-9 months after remission`,`Activating; adolescents 10 mg then 20 mg`],[`Venlafaxine XR (SNRI)`,`75 mg oral OD, up to 225 mg`,`Second line after SSRI failure`,`Raised BP; severe discontinuation syndrome`],[`Duloxetine (SNRI)`,`60 mg oral OD, max 120 mg`,`Second line; good with pain`,`Raised BP; avoid in liver disease, heavy drinkers`],[`Mirtazapine`,`15 mg oral at night, up to 45 mg`,`Switch or add-on option`,`Sedation, weight gain; rare agranulocytosis`],[`Amitriptyline (TCA)`,`25-50 mg at night, up to 75-150 mg`,`Rarely used now`,`Anticholinergic; lethal in overdose; avoid at suicide risk`],[`Bupropion XL`,`150 mg oral morning, up to 300 mg`,`Second line`,`No sexual dysfunction; contraindicated in seizures`]]},{heading:`PHQ-9 score and the action`,columns:[`PHQ-9`,`Severity`,`Action`],rows:[[`0-4`,`None or minimal`,`No treatment; rescreen later`],[`5-9`,`Mild`,`Behavioural activation, exercise; review in 2 weeks`],[`10-14`,`Moderate`,`SSRI plus psychological support`],[`15-19`,`Moderately severe`,`SSRI without delay; suicide enquiry every visit`],[`20-27`,`Severe`,`SSRI, same-day suicide assessment, involve family, refer`]]},{heading:`Suicide risk and the response`,columns:[`Risk level`,`Features`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Low`,`Fleeting passive thoughts, no plan`,`Treat, safety plan, weekly review`],[`Moderate`,`Active ideation without plan or intent`,`Remove means, written safety plan, review 48-72 hours`],[`High`,`Plan, means, intent or recent attempt`,`Do not leave alone; same-day psychiatry; admit`]]}],remember:[`**PHQ-2 screens, PHQ-9 grades**; 10 or more is the drug threshold.`,`Response is a 50% fall in PHQ-9; remission is a score under 5.`,`Asking about suicide does not plant the idea; never use SAD PERSONS to decide discharge.`,`**Never a TCA at suicide risk**: a fortnight's supply can kill.`,`Sertraline for the heart, pregnancy and breastfeeding; mirtazapine for insomnia; duloxetine for pain.`,`Screen for mania before every antidepressant.`,`Serotonin syndrome: clonus and fever after adding tramadol, linezolid or a triptan to an SSRI.`,`Pseudodementia lifts with treatment; dementia does not.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NCRB ADSI 2023 (released 2025): 1,71,418 suicides in 2023, rate 12.3 per lakh, about 73% men - replaces the 2022 figure.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - depression, PHQ-9 and suicide risk`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**5 of 9 symptoms for at least 2 weeks**, one being **depressed mood or anhedonia**, with distress or impairment, not due to a substance or medical illness, and **never mania or hypomania** (else bipolar). [DSM-5-TR]`],[`Relevant classification`,`Severity **mild, moderate, severe, severe with psychotic features** (ICD-11); NICE 2022: **less severe (PHQ-9 under 16) vs more severe (16 or more)**; specifiers - melancholic, psychotic, anxious distress, **peripartum onset**, seasonal; **dysthymia 2 years**; prolonged grief beyond 12 months.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Heritability **35-40%**, childhood adversity, recent loss or humiliation, chronic illness (diabetes, stroke, cancer), postpartum, alcohol, drugs (**steroids, isotretinoin, interferon, levetiracetam, reserpine**), hypothyroidism, B12 deficiency; India **2.7% current, treatment gap over 85%**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Stress -> **CRH-cortisol overdrive** -> low **BDNF**, hippocampal loss + low **5-HT/NA/DA** + inflammation (IL-6, kynurenine) -> amygdala over-, DLPFC under-activity -> mood, anhedonia, sleep change; **5-HT1A autoreceptor desensitisation explains the 2-4 week lag**.`],[`Key history`,`**PHQ-2 (3 or more) -> PHQ-9**; nine symptoms in the patient's idiom, duration, **function**; **graded suicide questions**; **screen for mania** before any antidepressant; psychotic features; past episodes and response; alcohol; drugs that interact (**tramadol, triptans, linezolid**); postpartum thoughts of harming baby.`],[`Key examination`,`MSE: poor eye contact, **psychomotor retardation**, low monotonous speech, restricted affect, hopelessness, guilt, **mood-congruent or nihilistic (Cotard) delusions**; 'I don't know' answers (pseudodementia); **self-harm scars, ligature marks**, thyroid, BMI, neurological screen if onset after 60.`],[`Investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis; **CBC, glucose/HbA1c, TSH** each first episode; **sodium at baseline and 2-4 weeks if over 65**; B12, LFT, RFT as indicated; **ECG if QTc risk (over 450 men, 470 women)**; pregnancy test; **no routine CT/MRI, DST or genetic tests**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`PHQ-9: **0-4 none, 5-9 mild, 10-14 moderate, 15-19 moderately severe, 20-27 severe**; **drug threshold 10**; **response = 50% fall, remission = under 5**; EPDS **13 or more** postpartum.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Less severe: **behavioural activation, exercise, guided self-help, CBT/IPT/problem-solving**; moderate or worse: **escitalopram 10 mg OD (max 20; 10 over 65)** or **sertraline 50 mg OD (max 200)** plus psychological therapy; review at **1-2 weeks** (weekly if under 25).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Adequate trial **4 weeks** at dose (6-8 elderly); **under 20-25% better** -> optimise -> **switch** (SSRI, **venlafaxine XR 75-225 mg, duloxetine 60 mg, mirtazapine 15-45 mg**) or add mirtazapine; after 2 failures -> **aripiprazole 2-10 mg, quetiapine XR 150-300 mg, lithium 0.6-1.0 mmol/L**; psychotic -> antidepressant + antipsychotic or **ECT**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Modified ECT only** (anaesthesia + muscle relaxant, **MHCA 2017**): psychotic depression, **stupor, food refusal, high suicide risk** - fastest-acting treatment; **rTMS to left DLPFC** for resistant depression; **lithium needs levels** (0.6-1.0 mmol/L, 12 h post-dose).`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy/breastfeeding: sertraline**, avoid paroxetine (cardiac defects); **adolescents: fluoxetine 10 -> 20 mg**; **elderly: escitalopram 5 mg or sertraline 25 mg**, check sodium, no TCAs; cardiac disease - sertraline; hepatic - escitalopram max 10 mg, avoid duloxetine.`],[`Complications`,`Suicide; **serotonin syndrome** (clonus, fever - cyproheptadine 12 mg); **hyponatraemia (SIADH)**; bleeding with NSAIDs (add PPI); sexual dysfunction; **discontinuation syndrome**; antidepressant-induced mania; **TCA overdose - wide QRS, give IV sodium bicarbonate**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Plan + means + intent or recent attempt -> **same-day psychiatric assessment, do not leave alone**; psychosis; food refusal or stupor (**ECT**); bipolar features; **failure of two adequate trials**; child under 12; severe depression in pregnancy; postpartum psychosis.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**PHQ-9 at 4 weeks** and every visit; decide at **6-8 weeks**; continue **6-9 months after remission** (2 years after recurrence); **taper over at least 4 weeks**; BP on SNRIs, weight on mirtazapine; dispense **1-2 weeks' supply** at risk.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Tele-MANAS 14416**, DMHP free drugs, **NSPS 2022**; **MHCA 2017 s.115** decriminalises attempted suicide, **s.18** right to care, **s.89 supported admission up to 30 days**, ECT only modified; written **safety plan**; means restriction (pesticides).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NCRB 2023: 1,71,418 suicides (rate 12.3/lakh)**; NICE NG222 (2022) less/more severe split remains current; **NICE NG225: no risk scales (SAD PERSONS) to predict suicide** - needs-based assessment.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**PHQ-2 screens, PHQ-9 grades**; asking about suicide does not plant it; **never a TCA at suicide risk**; sertraline for heart, pregnancy, breastfeeding; mirtazapine for insomnia and weight loss; duloxetine for pain; pseudodementia lifts with treatment.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Depression in OPD - from screen to plan`,steps:[{label:`PHQ-2 positive (3 or more of 6)`,detail:`Unexplained bodily symptoms, frequent visits, chronic disease, postpartum`},{label:`PHQ-9, function, graded suicide enquiry`,detail:`Ideation, plan, means, intent, past attempt`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Plan, means or intent, psychosis, food refusal`,detail:`Do not leave alone; same-day psychiatric assessment or admission`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Screen for mania and organic mimics`,detail:`TSH, CBC, glucose; drug history; bipolar = no antidepressant alone`,tone:`decision`},{label:`PHQ-9 under 10: low-intensity care`,detail:`Behavioural activation, exercise, guided self-help; review 2 weeks`},{label:`PHQ-9 10 or more: SSRI plus therapy`,detail:`Escitalopram 10 mg or sertraline 50 mg OD; safety plan; Tele-MANAS 14416`},{label:`Remission (PHQ-9 under 5)`,detail:`Continue 6-9 months, then taper over 4 weeks or more`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Antidepressant escalation ladder`,steps:[{label:`SSRI at starting dose`,detail:`Escitalopram 10 mg (5 mg elderly) or sertraline 50 mg; review 1-2 weeks`},{label:`Optimise dose after 4 weeks`,detail:`Under 20-25% better: check adherence, alcohol; escitalopram 20 mg, sertraline up to 200 mg`},{label:`Switch class`,detail:`Venlafaxine XR 75-225 mg, duloxetine 60 mg or mirtazapine 15-45 mg; cross-taper 1-2 weeks`},{label:`Augment (after two failed trials, specialist)`,detail:`Aripiprazole 2-10 mg, quetiapine XR 150-300 mg or lithium 0.6-1.0 mmol/L`,tone:`decision`},{label:`ECT`,detail:`Psychotic, stuporous, food refusal or high suicide risk; modified ECT only (MHCA)`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Choosing the drug by patient`,columns:[`Patient`,`Best choice`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Cardiac disease, pregnancy, breastfeeding`,`Sertraline 50 mg`,`TCAs, citalopram over 20 mg (QT), paroxetine in pregnancy`],[`Insomnia, poor appetite, weight loss`,`Mirtazapine 15 mg at night`,`Bupropion, fluoxetine (activating)`],[`Chronic pain, diabetic neuropathy`,`Duloxetine 60 mg`,`Duloxetine in liver disease or heavy drinking`],[`Adolescent`,`Fluoxetine 10 mg then 20 mg`,`Paroxetine, venlafaxine, TCAs`],[`Suicide risk`,`SSRI in 1-2 week supplies`,`TCAs (lethal in overdose)`]]}]},"psychiatry-anxiety":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`An **anxiety disorder** is fear or worry that is excessive, persistent and impairing.`,`It must not be due to a substance, a drug or a medical illness.`,`Anxiety affects about **3% of Indian adults**, women twice as often as men.`,`Patients present as **ghabrahat**, palpitations, gas or giddiness, rarely as anxiety.`,`Depression coexists in over half, so every anxious patient gets a suicide question.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**GAD**: uncontrollable worry about several areas for **at least 6 months**.`,`GAD also needs **3 of 6** symptoms: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, tension, poor sleep.`,`**Panic attack**: intense fear **peaking within about 10 minutes** with 4 of 13 symptoms.`,`**Panic disorder**: recurrent unexpected attacks plus **1 month** of worry or avoidance.`,`**Agoraphobia**: fear of 2 of 5 situations where escape is hard, for 6 months.`,`Social anxiety and specific phobia complete the group; **OCD and PTSD** are now separate chapters.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The **amygdala** is hyper-reactive and fires to harmless or ambiguous cues.`,`The medial prefrontal cortex fails to calm the amygdala, so worry runs on.`,`Amygdala output triggers a **noradrenaline surge** from the locus coeruleus: palpitations, tremor, sweating.`,`In panic, a brainstem **suffocation alarm** is oversensitive to carbon dioxide.`,`**Hyperventilation** causes respiratory alkalosis, low ionised calcium, tingling and carpopedal spasm.`,`A harmless sensation is **catastrophically misinterpreted** as a heart attack, and fear feeds the symptom.`,`**Avoidance** stops the fear from extinguishing, so avoidance spreads; graded exposure breaks this.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Screen with the **GAD-2**; a score of **3 or more** leads to the full GAD-7.`,`Suspect anxiety after **repeated visits and normal tests** for palpitations, chest tightness or giddiness.`,`Panic builds over minutes and fades in 20-30; **abrupt on-off palpitations over 150/min** suggest SVT.`,`Ask if attacks are unexpected or cued by crowds, scrutiny or one object.`,`Ask about **alcohol, alprazolam, caffeine** and morning tremor relieved by a drink or tablet.`,`Check drugs: **salbutamol, theophylline, excess thyroxine**, and tramadol or triptans before an SSRI.`,`Ask about past elated mood, because an SSRI can precipitate mania.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for **sweaty palms, tremor, sighing respiration** and restlessness.`,`Affect is anxious but reactive; a flat or sad affect suggests coexisting depression.`,`**Derealisation** during panic is common and is not psychosis.`,`Check pulse, BP and weight, and look for **goitre, lid lag and fine tremor**.`,`During an attack, check **SpO2 and capillary glucose**.`,`Look for signs of **alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal**; these patients need detoxification, not an SSRI.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Anxiety is a **clinical diagnosis**; tests exclude mimics and are done **once**.`,`**TSH** in every new case: hyperthyroidism is the commonest treatable mimic.`,`**Haemoglobin and blood glucose**, especially in patients on insulin or a sulphonylurea.`,`**ECG** for palpitations, chest pain, syncope or a first attack over 45.`,`Plasma metanephrines only for paroxysmal hypertension with headache and sweating.`,`Then stop testing and **write the diagnosis down**; repeated tests make panic chronic.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Name the illness confidently and explain the **fight-or-flight response**.`,`**CBT** works as well as drugs, and its benefit lasts after stopping.`,`**Graded exposure**: climb a written hierarchy, staying until anxiety halves.`,`**Slow breathing**: in for 4, out for 6, at 6-8 breaths a minute.`,`Drug threshold is **GAD-7 of 10 or more** with impairment; an SSRI is the long-term drug.`,`Start at **half the depression dose**: escitalopram 5 mg or sertraline 25 mg for 1 week.`,`Warn about **first-week jitteriness**; judge the full effect at **8-12 weeks**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Under-dosing explains most non-response; anxiety often needs the **upper dose**.`,`No response at 8-12 weeks: switch to another SSRI or to **venlafaxine XR**.`,`**Pregabalin** is the next option for GAD and acts within a week, but can be misused.`,`**Benzodiazepines** are only a **2-4 week bridge** with a written stop date.`,`**Avoid alprazolam**: it causes the commonest iatrogenic dependence in India.`,`**Propranolol** before a performance blocks tremor and palpitations but not worry.`,`Continue the SSRI **12 months after response**, then taper over 4-8 weeks.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy and breastfeeding**: CBT first; if a drug is needed, sertraline.`,`Avoid regular benzodiazepines near term because of floppy infant and neonatal withdrawal.`,`**Elderly**: start escitalopram 5 mg or sertraline 25 mg, check sodium, avoid benzodiazepines.`,`**Children**: CBT first; fluoxetine or sertraline only under specialist advice.`,`Reduce the pregabalin dose in patients with **renal impairment**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`First panic-like attack over 45, exertional chest pain or syncope: **rule out cardiac disease**.`,`Weight loss, tremor and goitre: **thyrotoxicosis**; send TSH before an SSRI.`,`Morning tremor relieved by alcohol or a tablet: withdrawal, which can cause seizures.`,`Escalating benzodiazepine doses or early refills mean **dependence**; refer for a supervised taper.`,`Refer after **two failed adequate trials**, or for OCD, PTSD, bipolar features or suicidal ideation.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anxiolytic drugs - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Escitalopram (SSRI)`,`5 mg oral OD for 1 week, then 10-20 mg`,`12 months after response`,`Early jitteriness; max 10 mg over 65 (QT)`],[`Sertraline (SSRI)`,`25 mg oral OD for 1 week, then 50-200 mg`,`12 months after response`,`Loose stools; choice in heart disease, pregnancy`],[`Venlafaxine XR (SNRI)`,`37.5-75 mg oral OD, up to 225 mg`,`12 months after response`,`Raised BP; check BP each visit`],[`Pregabalin`,`75 mg oral BD, up to 300 mg BD`,`Third line for GAD`,`Dizziness, weight gain, misuse; reduce in renal failure`],[`Clonazepam`,`0.25-0.5 mg oral BD`,`2-4 weeks only`,`Sedation, falls, dependence; no driving`],[`Lorazepam`,`1 mg oral once for a severe attack`,`Single dose`,`Never a repeat prescription from casualty`],[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg oral, 30-60 min before performance`,`As needed`,`Contraindicated in asthma and heart block`],[`Buspirone`,`5 mg oral BD-TDS, up to 30-60 mg/day`,`Takes 2-4 weeks`,`No dependence; useless for panic`]]},{heading:`Types of anxiety disorder`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Core feature`,`Duration`,`First-line treatment`],rows:[[`Generalised anxiety disorder`,`Uncontrollable worry plus 3 of 6 symptoms`,`6 months or more`,`CBT; SSRI or SNRI`],[`Panic disorder`,`Unexpected attacks peaking within minutes`,`1 month of worry or avoidance`,`CBT; SSRI; short benzodiazepine bridge`],[`Agoraphobia`,`Fear of 2 of 5 situations`,`6 months`,`Graded exposure; SSRI if panic`],[`Social anxiety disorder`,`Fear of scrutiny and embarrassment`,`6 months`,`CBT; SSRI; propranolol for one performance`],[`Specific phobia`,`Fear of one object or situation`,`6 months`,`Exposure therapy`]]},{heading:`GAD-7 score and the action`,columns:[`GAD-7`,`Severity`,`Action`],rows:[[`0-4`,`Minimal`,`Reassure; cut caffeine and alcohol`],[`5-9`,`Mild`,`Psychoeducation, breathing, exercise; review 4 weeks`],[`10-14`,`Moderate`,`CBT plus SSRI at half dose; review 2 weeks`],[`15-21`,`Severe`,`SSRI or SNRI plus CBT; refer if no response by 12 weeks`]]}],remember:[`**GAD-2 screens, GAD-7 grades**; 10 or more is the treatment threshold.`,`GAD lasts 6 months; panic disorder needs 1 month of fear after attacks.`,`Start the SSRI at **half dose** and warn about first-week jitteriness.`,`Benzodiazepines: a 2-4 week bridge, never alprazolam, never in alcohol users or the elderly.`,`Slow breathing, not a paper bag, for hyperventilation.`,`TSH, glucose, haemoglobin and ECG once, then write the diagnosis down.`,`Continue the SSRI 12 months after response; relapse is near 50% if stopped early.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - anxiety disorders and panic`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Excessive, persistent fear or worry** out of proportion to threat, with autonomic arousal and avoidance causing impairment; **GAD = uncontrollable worry 6 months + 3 of 6 symptoms**; **panic disorder = recurrent unexpected attacks + 1 month of worry or avoidance**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`DSM-5-TR/ICD-11: **GAD, panic disorder, agoraphobia (2 of 5 situations, 6 months), social anxiety, specific phobia**, separation anxiety; **OCD and PTSD are separate chapters**; panic attack = **4 of 13 symptoms peaking within about 10 minutes**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Heritability **30-40%**, behavioural inhibition, parental anxiety, adverse events, female (2:1); India about **3%** (NMHS); mimics - **thyrotoxicosis, hypoglycaemia, arrhythmia, phaeochromocytoma, caffeine, salbutamol, alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Hyper-reactive **amygdala** + weak **vmPFC** inhibition -> locus coeruleus NA surge, hypothalamic sympathetic output, HPA -> palpitations, tremor, worry; hyperventilation -> **respiratory alkalosis, low ionised Ca** -> tingling, carpopedal spasm; avoidance blocks extinction.`],[`Key history`,`**GAD-2 (3 or more) -> GAD-7**; how attacks start and end (**abrupt on-off over 150/min = SVT**), cued vs unexpected, avoidance and safety behaviours, **PHQ-9 and suicide question**, alcohol and alprazolam use, caffeine, thyroid symptoms, past elation.`],[`Key examination`,`Tense, sweaty palms, tremor, sighing; reactive anxious affect, intact cognition, good insight; **pulse, BP, thyroid signs, murmur**; in an attack **SpO2 and capillary glucose**; signs of withdrawal.`],[`Investigations`,`Once only: **TSH, Hb, glucose, ECG** (palpitations, chest pain, first attack over 45); Holter for on-off palpitations; sodium over 65 on SSRI; metanephrines only for paroxysmal hypertension; **stop investigating and write the diagnosis down**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`GAD-7: **0-4 minimal, 5-9 mild, 10-14 moderate, 15-21 severe**; **cut-off 10** (sensitivity 89%, specificity 82%); response = 50% fall, remission = under 5.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Stepped care: education -> guided self-help -> **CBT (GAD 12-15 sessions) or SSRI**; **escitalopram 5 mg OD x 1 week -> 10-20 mg** or **sertraline 25 mg x 1 week -> 50-200 mg**; slow breathing **6-8/min**, graded exposure, worry postponement.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`No response at **8-12 weeks** on full dose -> switch SSRI or **SNRI (venlafaxine XR 75-225 mg, duloxetine 60-120 mg)** -> **pregabalin 75 mg BD up to 300 mg BD**; buspirone 5 mg BD-TDS (2-4 weeks to act, useless in panic); hydroxyzine 25 mg up to QDS short term (max 100 mg/day); refer after two trials.`],[`Treatment - bridge & situational drugs`,`**Benzodiazepine only as a 2-4 week bridge** with a written stop date - clonazepam 0.25-0.5 mg BD or lorazepam 0.5-1 mg; **never alprazolam**; **propranolol 10-40 mg 30-60 min before performance** only; taper benzodiazepines **10-25% every 2-4 weeks** via diazepam equivalents (diazepam 10 = lorazepam 1 = clonazepam 0.5 = alprazolam 0.5 mg).`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: CBT first, sertraline**, avoid paroxetine and regular benzodiazepines near term; **elderly: escitalopram 5 mg**, avoid benzodiazepines; **renal: reduce pregabalin**; children: CBT first, low-dose sertraline or fluoxetine with weekly review.`],[`Complications`,`Agoraphobia, depression (over half), **benzodiazepine dependence**, alcohol misuse, suicide risk with panic plus depression, SSRI jitteriness in week 1, hyponatraemia, bleeding with NSAIDs.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**First attack over 45, exertional chest pain, syncope, abnormal ECG** -> cardiac work-up; on-off palpitations over 150 -> SVT; withdrawal tremor relieved by drink; suicidal ideation; escalating benzodiazepine use; OCD, PTSD, bipolar features.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **1-2 weeks** after SSRI start; **GAD-7 at 4 weeks**, judge at **8-12 weeks**; continue **12 months after response**, taper over **4-8 weeks**; BP on SNRIs, sodium in elderly, count benzodiazepine tablets.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Tele-MANAS 14416**, DMHP free SSRIs and counselling; cut caffeine, energy drinks, nicotine and alcohol; 30 minutes brisk walking most days.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change: **NICE CG113 (updated 2020)** stepped care, SSRI first-line drug and no long-term benzodiazepines remain current as of 2026.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Start the SSRI at half the depression dose**; slow breathing, **not a paper bag**; propranolol does nothing for worry and is contraindicated in asthma; buspirone useless for panic; graded exposure works only if the patient stays until anxiety halves.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Panic-like episode - assess and act`,steps:[{label:`Pulse, BP, SpO2, capillary glucose, ECG`,detail:`Exclude ACS, arrhythmia, hypoglycaemia, hypoxia first`},{label:`First attack over 45, chest pain, syncope, abnormal ECG?`,detail:`Yes = cardiac work-up, not anxiety`,tone:`decision`},{label:`On-off palpitations over 150/min or withdrawal tremor`,detail:`SVT recording; supervised detoxification`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Name it confidently; slow breathing 4 in, 6 out`,detail:`Grounding 5-4-3; no paper bag; lorazepam 1 mg oral only if extreme`},{label:`GAD-7, PHQ-9 and suicide question`,detail:`Diagnosis in writing; start SSRI at half dose`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Follow-up within 1 week; CBT with exposure`,detail:`Prevents agoraphobia`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Drug ladder for GAD and panic disorder`,steps:[{label:`CBT, self-help, slow breathing, exposure`,detail:`GAD-7 5-9; 12-15 sessions for GAD`},{label:`SSRI at half dose, then full dose`,detail:`Escitalopram 5 -> 10-20 mg; sertraline 25 -> 50-200 mg; judge at 8-12 weeks`},{label:`Switch SSRI or to SNRI`,detail:`Venlafaxine XR 75-225 mg; duloxetine 60 mg (max 120); check BP`},{label:`Pregabalin`,detail:`75 mg BD up to 300 mg BD; misuse risk; reduce in renal impairment`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Specialist: augmentation or specialist CBT`,detail:`After two adequate trials; benzodiazepine only a 2-4 week bridge`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Which anxiety disorder? Core feature, duration, first line`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Core feature`,`Duration`,`First line`],rows:[[`GAD`,`Uncontrollable worry, many domains + 3 of 6 symptoms`,`6 months`,`CBT or SSRI; SNRI; pregabalin third`],[`Panic disorder`,`Unexpected attacks, 4 of 13 symptoms, peak in minutes`,`1 month of worry or avoidance`,`CBT with interoceptive exposure; SSRI at half dose`],[`Agoraphobia`,`Fear of 2 of 5 situations where escape is hard`,`6 months`,`Graded exposure; SSRI if panic`],[`Social anxiety`,`Fear of scrutiny - blushing, tremor, avoids speaking`,`6 months`,`CBT, SSRI; propranolol 10-40 mg only before a performance`],[`Specific phobia`,`One cue - heights, animals, blood-injection`,`6 months`,`Exposure therapy; drugs rarely needed`]]}]},"psychiatry-alcohol-use-disorder":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Alcohol use disorder** is 2 or more of **11 DSM-5-TR criteria within 12 months**.`,`A **standard drink is about 10 g** of ethanol; one 30 mL peg of whisky is one drink.`,`**Hazardous drinking** is over 14 drinks a week for men or 7 for women.`,`A binge is more than 4 drinks (men) or 3 (women) on one occasion.`,`About **19% of Indian men** drink, and over 85% with the disorder are untreated.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Severity by criteria: **mild 2-3, moderate 4-5, severe 6 or more**.`,`Severe AUD corresponds roughly to ICD-11 **alcohol dependence**.`,`AUDIT zones: **0-7 low risk, 8-15 hazardous, 16-19 harmful, 20-40 possible dependence**.`,`**AUDIT-C** is positive at **4 or more in men** and 3 or more in women.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Alcohol **boosts GABA-A** inhibition and **blocks NMDA** glutamate receptors.`,`It releases endorphins that drive dopamine reward; **naltrexone** blocks this.`,`With daily drinking, GABA-A receptors fall and NMDA receptors rise: **tolerance**.`,`Stopping suddenly leaves **too little GABA and too much glutamate**: withdrawal.`,`Each withdrawal lowers the threshold for the next (**kindling**), so past seizures predict more.`,`Benzodiazepines are **cross-tolerant at GABA-A** and replace the missing inhibition.`,`Thiamine is used up by a glucose load, causing **Wernicke encephalopathy** in the mammillary bodies.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Screen every adult man and at-risk woman with **AUDIT-C**.`,`Screen in resistant hypertension, new AF, raised GGT, macrocytosis, injuries and TB defaulters.`,`Quantify the drinking: type, number of pegs, days a week, converted to standard drinks.`,`Ask the **time of the last drink** and about the **morning eye-opener**.`,`**Past withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens** mean inpatient detoxification.`,`Assess the **stage of change**, from pre-contemplation to maintenance.`,`Interview the **spouse** with consent, as the drinker minimises.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`In withdrawal look for **sweating, restlessness and tremor** of outstretched hands.`,`Hallucinations with a **clear sensorium** mean alcoholic hallucinosis; with confusion, delirium tremens.`,`**Wernicke check**: nystagmus or eye palsy, ataxia, confusion; one feature is enough.`,`Look for stigmata of liver disease: jaundice, palmar erythema, spider naevi, asterixis.`,`Look for **head injury** in every confused drinker, because subdural haematoma is common.`,`Score the **CIWA-Ar** (maximum 67) at baseline and repeatedly during withdrawal.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**GGT** rises with heavy drinking and falls within 2-6 weeks of abstinence.`,`**MCV over 100** supports heavy drinking and normalises within 3 months.`,`**AST:ALT above 2** suggests alcoholic liver disease; check albumin and INR.`,`Glucose, sodium, potassium, **magnesium** and phosphate before detoxification.`,`Creatinine decides acamprosate; LFT decides naltrexone.`,`**CT head** for a first, focal or late seizure, head injury or persistent confusion.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Brief intervention** for AUDIT 8-19 using **FRAMES**, taking 5-10 minutes.`,`**Abstinence** is the goal in dependence, liver disease, pregnancy and epilepsy.`,`Never tell a dependent drinker to stop abruptly without cover.`,`Home detox only if **CIWA-Ar under 15**, no past seizure or DT, and a reliable adult.`,`Use a **long-acting benzodiazepine** taper over 5-7 days: chlordiazepoxide or diazepam.`,`Use **lorazepam** in liver disease and the elderly, as it does not accumulate.`,`**Thiamine before glucose, always**: 100 mg IM or IV daily for 3-5 days.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Start a relapse-prevention drug **before discharge**; over 70% relapse within a year otherwise.`,`**Naltrexone** reduces heavy drinking; avoid in liver failure or with opioids.`,`**Acamprosate** supports abstinence and is **safe in liver disease**.`,`**Disulfiram** only for a motivated patient, supervised by a relative, with consent.`,`**Baclofen** is the anti-craving choice in **cirrhosis**.`,`Add motivational interviewing, CBT and **Alcoholics Anonymous**, with Al-Anon for the family.`,`Re-assess depression after **2-4 weeks of abstinence**; most secondary low mood lifts.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Withdrawal seizure** at 12-48 hours: IV lorazepam 2-4 mg; **do not start phenytoin**.`,`**Delirium tremens** at 48-96 hours: confusion, hallucinations, fever and autonomic storm.`,`Untreated DT mortality is **up to 25%**, falling to 1-5% with treatment.`,`In DT, load benzodiazepine to light sedation and give **thiamine 500 mg IV three times daily**.`,`Haloperidol is **only an adjunct** in DT; alone it lowers the seizure threshold.`,`Jaundice, ascites, haematemesis or asterixis means decompensated liver disease: admit.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`During home detox, contact the patient **daily** and rescore CIWA-Ar.`,`After detox, review weekly for a month, then monthly for 6-12 months.`,`Repeat GGT and MCV at 4-8 weeks and show the patient the fall.`,`Treat a **lapse as a learning event** and restart the plan the same week.`,`Refer for past seizures or DT, Wernicke-Korsakoff, decompensated liver disease or polysubstance use.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Alcohol withdrawal and relapse-prevention drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Chlordiazepoxide`,`50 mg oral QDS day 1, tapering`,`Stop after day 6`,`Sedation; accumulates in liver disease`],[`Diazepam`,`10-20 mg oral QDS day 1, cut 20-25% daily`,`5-7 days`,`Sedation; withhold if drowsy`],[`Lorazepam`,`1-2 mg oral 6-8 hourly; 2-4 mg IV for seizures`,`Taper over 5-7 days`,`Choice in liver disease and elderly`],[`Thiamine`,`100 mg IM or IV daily`,`3-5 days in every detox`,`Give before glucose; rare anaphylaxis`],[`Thiamine for Wernicke`,`500 mg IV three times daily`,`2-3 days, then 250 mg daily`,`Correct magnesium too`],[`Naltrexone`,`25 mg oral OD for 3 days, then 50 mg OD`,`3-6 months or longer`,`Not in liver failure or with opioids`],[`Acamprosate`,`666 mg oral TDS if 60 kg or more`,`6-12 months`,`Diarrhoea; avoid if CrCl under 30`],[`Disulfiram`,`250 mg oral OD, 24 h after last drink`,`Supervised, long term`,`Not in heart disease or psychosis; hepatotoxic`],[`Baclofen`,`5 mg oral TDS, up to 10-20 mg TDS`,`Long term`,`Sedation; taper to stop`]]},{heading:`Alcohol withdrawal timeline`,columns:[`Time from last drink`,`Syndrome`,`Key feature`,`Action`],rows:[[`6-12 hours`,`Minor withdrawal`,`Tremor, sweating, anxiety`,`CIWA-Ar, benzodiazepine taper, thiamine`],[`12-24 hours`,`Alcoholic hallucinosis`,`Hallucinations, clear sensorium`,`Increase benzodiazepine`],[`12-48 hours`,`Withdrawal seizures`,`Generalised, single or brief cluster`,`IV lorazepam; no phenytoin`],[`48-96 hours`,`Delirium tremens`,`Confusion, fever, autonomic storm`,`Admit; load benzodiazepine; IV thiamine`]]},{heading:`AUDIT score and the action`,columns:[`AUDIT`,`Zone`,`Action`],rows:[[`0-7`,`Low risk`,`Education; rescreen yearly`],[`8-15`,`Hazardous`,`Brief intervention (FRAMES)`],[`16-19`,`Harmful`,`Brief intervention, counselling, GGT follow-up`],[`20-40`,`Possible dependence`,`Assisted withdrawal, relapse-prevention drug, refer`]]}],remember:[`**FRAMES**: Feedback, Responsibility, Advice, Menu, Empathy, Self-efficacy.`,`Withdrawal clock: tremor 6-12 h, hallucinosis 12-24 h, seizures 12-48 h, DT 48-96 h.`,`**Thiamine before glucose**, always.`,`Treat Wernicke on any one feature: confusion, eye signs or ataxia.`,`Benzodiazepines prevent withdrawal seizures; phenytoin does not.`,`Lorazepam in liver disease; chlordiazepoxide or diazepam if the liver is normal.`,`Naltrexone for heavy drinking, acamprosate in liver disease, baclofen in cirrhosis.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - alcohol use disorder and withdrawal`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Problematic drinking with **2 or more of 11 DSM-5-TR criteria in 12 months** - **mild 2-3, moderate 4-5, severe 6 or more**; ICD-11 harmful use and **alcohol dependence**; standard drink **10 g ethanol** (30 mL spirits).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**AUDIT zones: 0-7 low, 8-15 hazardous, 16-19 harmful, 20-40 possible dependence**; **AUDIT-C positive 4 or more men, 3 or more women**; hazardous = over **14 drinks/week men, 7 women**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Heritability **about 50%**, early onset, family history, peers, stress, availability; India **about 19% of men drink**, AUD **4.6%**, over 85% untreated; children of dependent parents 3-4 times risk.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Chronic ethanol -> **GABA-A downregulation + NMDA upregulation** -> abrupt stop -> **low GABA, excess glutamate, NA surge** -> tremor, seizures (kindling), DT; low thiamine -> **Wernicke** (mammillary bodies) -> Korsakoff; ADH/ALDH -> acetaldehyde (disulfiram target).`],[`Key history`,`**AUDIT-C**, quantified history in pegs/standard drinks, **time of last drink, eye-opener**, **past withdrawal seizures or DT**, 11 criteria, stage of change, other substances, complications, mood and suicide, spouse interview, interacting drugs.`],[`Key examination`,`Tremor, sweating, tachycardia, **CIWA-Ar (10 items, max 67)**, orientation; **Wernicke check - nystagmus, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia**; liver stigmata, asterixis, neuropathy, head injury, capillary glucose.`],[`Investigations`,`**GGT, MCV over 100, AST:ALT over 2**, INR, albumin; **glucose, Na, K, Mg, phosphate, creatinine**; CBC, lipase; USG and **FibroScan (over 16 kPa = cirrhosis)**; ECG; **CT head for first, focal or late seizure**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Withdrawal clock: **tremor 6-12 h, hallucinosis 12-24 h (clear sensorium), seizures 12-48 h, DT 48-96 h (clouded, autonomic storm)**; CIWA-Ar (max 67) **under 8-10 mild, 8-15 moderate, over 15 severe** - **15 or more = inpatient detox**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`AUDIT 8-19: **brief intervention (FRAMES)**; dependence: assisted withdrawal - **chlordiazepoxide 50 mg QDS day 1 tapering to stop by day 7** or **diazepam 10-20 mg QDS reducing 20-25% daily**; **lorazepam 1-2 mg 6-8 h in liver disease/elderly**; **thiamine 100 mg IM/IV daily 3-5 days before glucose**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Relapse prevention before discharge: **naltrexone 25 mg x 3 days -> 50 mg OD 3-6 months** (no opioids, LFT); **acamprosate 666 mg TDS (1332 mg/day under 60 kg) 6-12 months** (CrCl over 30); **disulfiram 250 mg OD supervised**; **baclofen 5 -> 10-20 mg TDS in cirrhosis**; topiramate, gabapentin off-label.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Home detox only if**: no past seizure or DT, CIWA-Ar under 15, no severe illness or polysubstance use, a relative holds tablets, **daily contact**; skip the dose if drowsy; symptom-triggered dosing (CIWA-Ar 10 or more) where nursing allows.`],[`Special groups`,`**Liver disease: lorazepam/oxazepam, acamprosate or baclofen, avoid naltrexone in failure**; elderly: lorazepam; **pregnancy: no safe amount (FASD)**, inpatient detox, avoid disulfiram; renal: avoid acamprosate if CrCl under 30.`],[`Complications`,`**Withdrawal seizures, DT (1-5% mortality treated, up to 25% untreated)**, **Wernicke-Korsakoff**, alcoholic hepatitis and cirrhosis, pancreatitis, AF, cardiomyopathy, neuropathy, subdural haematoma, depression and suicide.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Any one Wernicke feature -> thiamine 500 mg IV TDS 2-3 days then 250 mg daily 3-5 days**; DT; seizure; past DT; decompensated liver; first or focal seizure (image); polysubstance; suicide risk; pregnancy; adolescents.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Daily during home detox; weekly for 1 month, then monthly **6-12 months**; **GGT normalises 2-6 weeks, MCV 3 months** - use as feedback; LFT on naltrexone/disulfiram, creatinine on acamprosate; lapse = learning event.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Screen all adults with AUDIT-C; DDTCs, **DMHP, Tele-MANAS 14416**, AA and Al-Anon; vaccinate **hepatitis B/A, pneumococcus**; treat tobacco at the same time; MHCA 2017 right to care.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change: **NICE CG115/CG100, ASAM 2020 withdrawal guideline** and EASL 2018 remain the cited current standards as of 2026.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Thiamine before glucose, always**; benzodiazepines prevent seizures and DT, **phenytoin does not**; haloperidol only as an adjunct in DT; naltrexone for heavy drinking, acamprosate for abstinence and liver disease, baclofen in cirrhosis, disulfiram only supervised and never covertly.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dependent drinker who wants to stop - detox pathway`,steps:[{label:`AUDIT 20 or more / severe AUD`,detail:`Time of last drink; past seizures or DT; CIWA-Ar`},{label:`Past seizure or DT, CIWA-Ar 15+, polysubstance?`,detail:`Yes = inpatient detox; no carer or severe illness = admit`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any of confusion, eye signs, ataxia`,detail:`Wernicke: thiamine 500 mg IV TDS 2-3 days, correct magnesium`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Thiamine 100 mg IM/IV daily 3-5 days`,detail:`Before any glucose; then oral thiamine and folic acid 5 mg`},{label:`Long-acting benzodiazepine taper 5-7 days`,detail:`Chlordiazepoxide 50 mg QDS day 1; lorazepam if liver disease`},{label:`Relapse-prevention drug plus psychosocial support`,detail:`Naltrexone, acamprosate or supervised disulfiram before discharge`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Withdrawal severity ladder - time and treatment`,steps:[{label:`Minor withdrawal (6-12 h)`,detail:`Tremor, sweating, anxiety; benzodiazepine taper + thiamine`},{label:`Alcoholic hallucinosis (12-24 h)`,detail:`Clear sensorium; raise benzodiazepine; antipsychotic only if persistent`},{label:`Withdrawal seizure (12-48 h)`,detail:`Lorazepam 2-4 mg IV; no phenytoin; CT if first, focal or late`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Delirium tremens (48-96 h)`,detail:`Admit HDU; diazepam 10-20 mg IV every 5-10 min to light sedation; thiamine 500 mg IV TDS`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Anti-craving drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose`,`Best for`,`Avoid in`],rows:[[`Naltrexone`,`25 mg x 3 days, then 50 mg OD`,`Reducing heavy drinking, normal liver`,`Opioid use, acute hepatitis, liver failure`],[`Acamprosate`,`666 mg TDS (1332 mg/day under 60 kg)`,`Maintaining abstinence; liver disease`,`CrCl under 30 mL/min`],[`Disulfiram`,`250 mg OD, 24 h after last drink, supervised`,`Motivated, abstinence goal, supervising relative`,`IHD, heart failure, psychosis, pregnancy`],[`Baclofen`,`5 mg TDS up to 10-20 mg TDS`,`Cirrhosis or alcohol-related liver disease`,`Renal impairment (reduce); never stop abruptly`]]}]},"psychiatry-somatic-symptom-disorder":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Somatic symptom disorder** is one or more distressing bodily symptoms lasting **more than 6 months**.`,`It also needs **disproportionate thoughts**, high health anxiety, or excessive time spent on the symptom.`,`It is a **positive diagnosis** based on the psychological response, not on unexplained symptoms.`,`It can coexist with real disease and can be made at the first visit.`,`Unexplained symptoms make up **15-30% of primary care consultations**.`,`Indian idioms of distress include body ache, burning feet, gas, kamzori and **dhat**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Severity: **mild** with one feature of criterion B, **moderate** with two or more.`,`**Severe** means two or more features plus many complaints or one very severe symptom.`,`ICD-11 calls it **bodily distress disorder**.`,`**Illness anxiety disorder** is fear of serious illness with minimal or no symptoms.`,`Somatic disorders are unintentional; **factitious disorder** is intentional, to gain the sick role.`,`**Malingering** is intentional for external gain and is not a mental disorder.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**The symptoms are real**: normal body signals are processed abnormally, not imagined.`,`The **anterior insula and cingulate** are overactive, so ordinary sensations feel loud.`,`A strong prior belief of illness overrides normal sensory input, as in the nocebo effect.`,`**Central sensitisation** and weak descending pain inhibition cause pain without new tissue damage.`,`**Catastrophic misinterpretation** leads to checking and reassurance-seeking, which reinforce the loop.`,`Rest and avoidance cause **deconditioning**, poor sleep and more pain.`,`Repeated tests and fragmented care by many doctors are the strongest drivers of chronicity.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Let the patient give the **full symptom list** in their own words at the first visit.`,`Ask the **specific fear**: what do you think is causing this?`,`Ask what has been happening in their life since the symptoms started.`,`Measure criterion B: hours spent checking, number of doctors and tests, days off work.`,`Screen everyone with **PHQ-9, GAD-7** and a direct suicide question.`,`Ask about analgesics, **tramadol, benzodiazepines** and injections already taken.`,`Ask **red-flag questions** each time: weight loss, fever, bleeding, night pain, focal weakness.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Examine carefully and visibly at every visit**; the examination itself is therapeutic.`,`Look for depressed or anxious mood hidden behind the bodily complaints.`,`Check the belief is **not delusional**: fixed infestation or rotting organs means psychosis.`,`**Hoover sign** and give-way weakness are positive signs of functional neurological disorder.`,`Dissociative seizures have **eyes closed**, side-to-side head movement and no post-ictal confusion.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Plan **one defined panel once** and say in advance what it will exclude.`,`Typical panel: CBC, ESR or CRP, glucose, TSH, renal and liver function, calcium, urinalysis.`,`Add **vitamin B12 and vitamin D** for fatigue, burning feet or body ache.`,`Do not repeat endoscopy, MRI or CT for the same complaint; incidental findings start new cycles.`,`**Write in the notes** what has been excluded and when.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**One doctor, one plan**: be the single point of contact.`,`**Timed appointments**: a fixed 15 minutes every 2-4 weeks, whether symptoms are bad or not.`,`**Reattribution** has three stages: feeling understood, broadening the agenda, making the link.`,`Give a **physiological explanation**, never 'it is all in your mind'.`,`**CBT** has the best evidence; graded activity and pacing build function.`,`Set **functional goals**, such as walking 20 minutes, rather than cure.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Drugs treat the **comorbidity and the pain**, not the belief; explain them honestly.`,`**SSRI** (escitalopram or sertraline) for coexisting depression, anxiety or illness anxiety.`,`**Amitriptyline 10 mg at night**, rising to 25-50 mg, for pain-predominant disorder.`,`**Duloxetine 60 mg** for functional pain or pain with depression.`,`**Do not prescribe** opioids, benzodiazepines, vitamin injections or tonics.`,`**Somatic delusions** need an antipsychotic such as risperidone 1-2 mg, and referral.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Dhat syndrome**: the belief that semen loss causes weakness; educate without ridicule.`,`In dhat, treat the underlying anxiety, depression or erectile difficulty.`,`**Elderly**: avoid TCAs and start escitalopram 5 mg.`,`**Pregnancy**: prefer sertraline; **children**: CBT and family work first.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Keep fixed reviews every 2-4 weeks, widening to 6-8 weeks as function improves.`,`Track **PHQ-15, PHQ-9 and GAD-7** at each visit.`,`Assess any **new symptom pattern on its merits**; these patients also get organic disease.`,`New multiple symptoms after **50** suggest occult disease, not a new somatic disorder.`,`Refer for suicidal ideation, somatic delusions, functional neurological disorder or refractory illness.`,`Refer **once with a specific question**, and take the patient back afterwards.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in somatic symptom disorder`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Escitalopram (SSRI)`,`5-10 mg oral OD, up to 20 mg`,`6-12 months after response`,`Early nausea and jitteriness; taper over 4 weeks`],[`Sertraline (SSRI)`,`25-50 mg oral OD, up to 200 mg`,`6-12 months after response`,`Loose stools; preferred in heart disease, pregnancy`],[`Fluoxetine (SSRI)`,`20-40 mg oral OD`,`6-12 months after response`,`Evidence in illness anxiety; activating`],[`Amitriptyline (TCA)`,`10 mg at night, up to 25-50 mg (max 75)`,`Works in 1-2 weeks for pain`,`Drowsiness, dry mouth; lethal in overdose`],[`Duloxetine (SNRI)`,`30 mg oral OD for 1-2 weeks, then 60 mg`,`Long term for pain`,`Nausea, raised BP; avoid in liver disease`],[`Risperidone`,`1-2 mg oral`,`Specialist-led`,`Only for somatic delusions, not SSD`]]},{heading:`Somatic and related disorders by intent`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Intentional?`,`Motivation`,`Management`],rows:[[`Somatic symptom disorder`,`No`,`None; distress is genuine`,`One doctor, timed visits, CBT`],[`Illness anxiety disorder`,`No`,`None`,`CBT, SSRI, reduce checking`],[`Functional neurological disorder`,`No`,`None`,`Show positive signs; physiotherapy`],[`Factitious disorder`,`Yes`,`To assume the sick role`,`Avoid confrontation; psychiatry`],[`Malingering`,`Yes`,`External gain (money, leave)`,`Not a mental disorder; document`]]},{heading:`PHQ-15 score and the action`,columns:[`PHQ-15`,`Somatic burden`,`Action`],rows:[[`0-4`,`Minimal`,`Explanation and reassurance`],[`5-9`,`Low`,`Positive explanation; PHQ-9 and GAD-7; review at 4 weeks`],[`10-14`,`Medium`,`Timed 2-4 weekly visits; treat comorbidity; graded activity`],[`15-30`,`High`,`One named doctor, drug as indicated, CBT, consider referral`]]}],remember:[`SSD is a **positive diagnosis**: disproportionate thoughts, anxiety or time for over 6 months.`,`The best single intervention is the **timed appointment** with one named doctor.`,`Give a mechanism, never a negation: 'I know what is causing this'.`,`Depression or anxiety coexists in **50-70%**; screen with PHQ-9 and GAD-7.`,`Reattribution: feel understood, broaden the agenda, make the link.`,`Unintentional is SSD; intentional for sick role is factitious; for gain is malingering.`,`Never opioids or benzodiazepines for functional symptoms.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - somatic symptom disorder and unexplained symptoms`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**One or more distressing somatic symptoms** with **disproportionate thoughts, persistent health anxiety or excessive time/energy** devoted to them, lasting **more than 6 months** - a **positive diagnosis**, not a diagnosis of exclusion; may coexist with real disease.`],[`Relevant classification`,`DSM-5-TR group: **SSD, illness anxiety disorder, functional neurological disorder, factitious disorder**; **malingering is not a mental disorder**; ICD-11 = **bodily distress disorder**; severity mild/moderate/severe; specifiers predominant pain, persistent.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Childhood adversity, illness in a relative, anxious temperament, stress (marital conflict, debt, violence), **fragmented care and repeated tests**; culture-bound idioms - **dhat**, burning feet, gas, kamzori; 15-30% of primary care consultations.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Expectation of illness (prior) + **insula/ACC hyper-salience** -> amplified normal sensations; **central sensitisation** (dorsal horn wind-up, weak PAG descending 5-HT/NA inhibition) -> pain; checking, avoidance, deconditioning and reassurance keep the loop going.`],[`Key history`,`Full symptom list in own words; **explanatory model and the specific fear**; life events since onset; **criterion B** (hours, doctors, tests); **PHQ-9, GAD-7, suicide question**; analgesic, tramadol, benzodiazepine use; collect old records; red-flag questions each visit.`],[`Key examination`,`**Examine visibly at every visit**; MSE for depression, anxiety, **somatic delusions** (infestation, rotting organs = psychosis); FND positive signs - **Hoover sign, give-way weakness, tremor entrainment**; dissociative seizures - eyes closed, side-to-side head movement, no post-ictal confusion.`],[`Investigations`,`**One defined panel once**: CBC, ESR/CRP, glucose/HbA1c, TSH, renal and liver function, calcium, urinalysis; B12, vitamin D if fits; **no repeat endoscopy, MRI or CT** without a new sign; document what is excluded.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`DSM-5-TR criteria A + B + C (over 6 months); **PHQ-15: 0-4 minimal, 5-9 low, 10-14 medium, 15-30 high** somatic burden.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**One named doctor**, **fixed time-based 15-minute reviews every 2-4 weeks**, **reattribution (Goldberg-Gask): feel understood -> broaden agenda -> make the link**, physiological explanation in writing, **CBT**, graded activity with pacing, reduce checking and reassurance-seeking.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Comorbid mood/anxiety: **escitalopram 5-10 mg (up to 20 mg)** or sertraline 25-200 mg, continue **6-12 months after response**; pain-predominant: **amitriptyline 10 mg up to 25-50 mg (max 75 mg) at night** or **duloxetine 30 -> 60 mg**; fluoxetine 20-40 mg for illness anxiety.`],[`Special groups`,`**Elderly: avoid TCAs, escitalopram 5 mg**; pregnancy: sertraline; children: CBT and family work; **dhat: sex education without ridicule**, treat anxiety, depression, PE or ED (sildenafil 50 mg).`],[`Complications`,`Iatrogenic harm from tests and surgery, **opioid (tramadol) and benzodiazepine dependence**, depression and suicide, disability, missed organic disease.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**New or changed symptom pattern**, weight loss, fever, bleeding, mass, focal signs, nocturnal pain, **first onset after 50**, somatic delusions, suicidal ideation; refer **once with a specific question**, FND for physiotherapy-led rehabilitation.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Keep timed visits, widen to 6-8 weeks as function improves; **brief examination, PHQ-15, PHQ-9, GAD-7** and functional goals each visit; withdraw analgesics and benzodiazepines gradually.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change: **NICE NG193 (2021)** - no opioids, gabapentinoids or long-term NSAIDs for chronic primary pain; antidepressants (amitriptyline, duloxetine, SSRIs) permitted - still current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Give a mechanism, never a negation**; the timed appointment is the core intervention; SSD unintentional, factitious intentional for sick role, malingering intentional for external gain; FND diagnosed by **positive signs**, not a normal MRI.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Patient with persistent unexplained symptoms`,steps:[{label:`Full history, fear, life events; visible examination`,detail:`Red flags each visit: weight loss, bleeding, focal signs`},{label:`Red flags or new pattern?`,detail:`Yes = investigate on merits like any patient`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fixed delusional conviction?`,detail:`Infestation, rotting organs = psychosis; antipsychotic, refer`,tone:`warn`},{label:`One defined panel, agreed in advance`,detail:`CBC, ESR/CRP, glucose, TSH, RFT, LFT, calcium, urine`},{label:`Reattribution and written explanation`,detail:`Feel understood -> broaden agenda -> make the link`},{label:`Timed reviews, CBT, graded activity, treat comorbidity`,detail:`SSRI, amitriptyline or duloxetine; goal is function`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Unexplained symptoms - classify by intent and motive`,root:`Bodily symptoms out of proportion`,arms:[{label:`Not intentional`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Somatic symptom disorder - disproportionate thoughts 6 months`,`Illness anxiety - fear of disease, minimal symptoms`,`Functional neurological disorder - Hoover sign`]},{label:`Intentional, internal motive`,tone:`decision`,steps:[`Factitious disorder - to assume the sick role`,`Avoid confrontation; limit tests; psychiatry`]},{label:`Intentional, external gain`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Malingering - money, leave, drugs, litigation`,`Not a mental disorder; document objectively`]}]}]},"psychiatry-tobacco-cessation":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Tobacco dependence** is a chronic relapsing disorder driven by nicotine.`,`**28.6% of Indian adults** use tobacco: 21.4% smokeless and 10.7% smoked.`,`Tobacco kills **over 1.3 million Indians a year**.`,`India leads the world in **oral cancer and oral submucous fibrosis** from smokeless tobacco and areca.`,`Withdrawal starts within **24 hours**, peaks at 2-3 days and settles over 2-4 weeks.`,`Help raises 1-year abstinence from **3-5% unaided to 25-30%**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Dependence is graded with the **Fagerstrom test (FTND, 0-10)**.`,`FTND: **0-2 very low, 3-4 low, 5 medium, 6-7 high, 8-10 very high**.`,`Two questions matter most: **time to first use** after waking and **number per day**.`,`First use **within 30 minutes** or **more than 10 a day** means a drug is needed.`,`Smoked forms include cigarette, **bidi** and hookah; smokeless include khaini, gutkha, zarda and mawa.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Smoked nicotine reaches the brain in **10-20 seconds**, the most reinforcing route.`,`Nicotine binds **alpha4-beta2 nicotinic receptors** on dopamine neurons of the VTA.`,`Dopamine release in the **nucleus accumbens** rewards each puff or chew.`,`Repeated use **increases the number of receptors**, which resensitise overnight.`,`That causes the craving for the **first cigarette of the morning**.`,`Cues like tea, meals and stress become linked to reward, so craving outlasts withdrawal.`,`Smoke hydrocarbons (not nicotine) **induce CYP1A2**, so some drug levels rise on quitting.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Ask every patient at every visit** and record tobacco as a vital sign.`,`Ask about **khaini, gutkha and paan** too; smokeless users say no to 'do you smoke'.`,`Quantify type, amount per day and duration; pack-years for cigarettes.`,`Assess readiness: are you willing to quit in the **next 30 days**?`,`Ask about past quit attempts; **under-dosed NRT** is the usual reason for failure.`,`Ask about seizures, eating disorder and alcohol withdrawal before bupropion.`,`Note **clozapine, olanzapine, theophylline and warfarin**, which need dose review on quitting.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for nicotine-stained fingers, **clubbing**, pallor and cachexia.`,`Record BP, BMI and waist to track weight gain after quitting.`,`**Examine the mouth** of every smokeless user under good light.`,`**Leukoplakia** is a white patch that cannot be scraped off.`,`**Erythroplakia**, a red velvety patch, carries the highest malignant risk.`,`**Submucous fibrosis** shows blanched mucosa, fibrous bands and mouth opening under 3 fingers.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Dependence needs **no test for diagnosis**; history and FTND are enough.`,`**Exhaled carbon monoxide** above about 10 ppm confirms recent smoking.`,`**Spirometry** in smokers over 35-40 with symptoms; FEV1/FVC under 0.7 confirms COPD.`,`Chest X-ray and **sputum CBNAAT** for cough over 2-3 weeks, haemoptysis or weight loss.`,`**Biopsy** any oral lesion persisting beyond 2-3 weeks after removing irritants.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Follow the **5 A's**: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange.`,`**Advise** in one strong, personal sentence; brief advice under 3 minutes adds 2-3% quitting.`,`**Assist**: set a quit date **within 2 weeks** and stop completely that day.`,`Teach the **4 D's** for craving: Delay, Deep breathing, Drink water, Distract.`,`Offer **varenicline or combination NRT** (patch plus gum or lozenge) to dependent users.`,`**Arrange** follow-up at week 1, week 2, 1 month and monthly for 3 months.`,`Give the **quitline 1800-11-2356** and the mCessation missed-call number.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`For the patient not ready, use the **5 R's**: Relevance, Risks, Rewards, Roadblocks, Repetition.`,`**Bupropion** suits the depressed quitter or one who fears weight gain.`,`**Cytisine** is now a WHO first-line option, cheap where available.`,`For the very dependent, combine varenicline or bupropion with NRT under close follow-up.`,`On quitting, **reduce clozapine and olanzapine by about 25-30%** and watch INR.`,`**E-cigarettes are banned in India** under the 2019 Act and cannot be recommended.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: behavioural support first, then intermittent NRT (gum or lozenge).`,`Varenicline and bupropion are **not used in pregnancy**.`,`**Heart disease**: NRT is safe in stable disease and far safer than tobacco.`,`Use only short-acting NRT under supervision within **2 weeks of MI** or in unstable angina.`,`**Smokeless users**: 4 mg gum or lozenge for high dependence, or varenicline.`,`**Adolescents**: counselling first; NRT from 12 years under supervision.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Under-dosing and stopping too early** cause most failures; raise NRT if craving persists.`,`Treat a **lapse as information**, not failure, so it does not become relapse.`,`Stop varenicline or bupropion the same day for new agitation, low mood or suicidal thoughts.`,`Refer urgently any oral ulcer over **3 weeks**, red patch, induration or restricted mouth opening.`,`**COTPA section 6** bans sale to under-18s and within 100 yards of schools.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Tobacco cessation drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Nicotine patch`,`21 mg/24 h (14 mg if 10 or fewer a day)`,`21 mg 6 weeks, 14 mg 2 weeks, 7 mg 2 weeks`,`Skin irritation, vivid dreams; rotate sites`],[`Nicotine gum`,`2 mg, or 4 mg if first use within 30 min`,`6-12 weeks, then taper`,`Chew and park; no tea or cola for 15 min`],[`Nicotine lozenge`,`2 mg or 4 mg, one every 1-2 hours`,`6-12 weeks, then taper`,`Dissolve slowly; do not chew or swallow`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg OD days 1-3, 0.5 mg BD days 4-7, then 1 mg BD`,`12 weeks, plus 12 if abstinent`,`Nausea in 30%; eGFR under 30 max 0.5 mg BD`],[`Bupropion SR`,`150 mg OD for 3 days, then 150 mg BD`,`7-12 weeks`,`Seizures 1 in 1000; not in epilepsy or eating disorder`],[`Cytisine`,`1.5 mg tablets, 6 a day tapering`,`25-day course`,`Nausea, dry mouth; quit by day 5`]]},{heading:`The 5 A's and the 5 R's`,columns:[`Step`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Ask`,`Record tobacco use, smoked and smokeless, at every visit`],[`Advise`,`One clear, strong, personal sentence to quit`],[`Assess`,`Willing to quit in 30 days? Score dependence`],[`Assist`,`Quit date within 2 weeks, drug, trigger plan`],[`Arrange`,`Follow up week 1, week 2, then monthly`],[`5 R's (not ready)`,`Relevance, Risks, Rewards, Roadblocks, Repetition`]]},{heading:`Choosing the drug`,columns:[`Patient`,`Best choice`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Highly dependent, cost allows`,`Varenicline or combination NRT`,`Single short-acting NRT alone`],[`Depression or fear of weight gain`,`Bupropion SR`,`Bupropion if seizures or eating disorder`],[`Pregnancy`,`Behavioural support, then gum or lozenge`,`Varenicline, bupropion`],[`Stable heart disease`,`NRT`,`No need to avoid NRT`],[`Smokeless tobacco user`,`4 mg gum or lozenge, or varenicline`,`Ignoring smokeless use`]]}],remember:[`**5 A's**: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange.`,`**5 R's**: Relevance, Risks, Rewards, Roadblocks, Repetition.`,`**4 D's** for craving: Delay, Deep breathing, Drink water, Distract.`,`First use within 30 minutes or more than 10 a day means pharmacotherapy.`,`Varenicline is an alpha4-beta2 partial agonist: it eases craving and blunts a lapse.`,`EAGLES found no excess serious neuropsychiatric events with varenicline or bupropion.`,`Quitting removes CYP1A2 induction: reduce clozapine and theophylline.`,`COTPA section 7 mandates pictorial warnings over 85% of the pack.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO Tobacco Cessation 2024: cytisine listed as a recommended first-line option alongside varenicline, NRT and bupropion (low-cost choice).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - tobacco cessation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Tobacco use disorder: 2 or more of 11 criteria in 12 months** (mild 2-3, moderate 4-5, severe 6+); ICD-11 nicotine dependence; withdrawal within 24 h, **peaks 2-3 days, settles 2-4 weeks**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Withdrawal = 4 of 7 within 24 h** of stopping: irritability, anxiety, poor concentration, **increased appetite**, restlessness, low mood, insomnia; **FTND 6+ or first use within 30 min / over 10 a day = high dependence -> drug**; exhaled CO over 10 ppm = smoking.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Dependence by **FTND (0-10): 0-2 very low, 3-4 low, 5 medium, 6-7 high, 8-10 very high**; **Heaviness of Smoking Index** - time to first use and number per day; smoked (cigarette, **bidi**, hookah) vs **smokeless** (khaini, gutkha, zarda, mawa, paan).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India **28.6% of adults use tobacco** (42.4% men, 14.2% women; **21.4% smokeless**) [GATS-2]; over 1.3 million deaths a year; mental illness, alcohol, peers, cheap availability.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Nicotine -> **alpha4-beta2 nAChR on VTA** -> **dopamine in nucleus accumbens** -> reward; chronic use -> receptor desensitisation and **upregulation** -> morning craving; smoke PAHs **induce CYP1A2**; smokeless nitrosamines -> oral cancer; areca nut -> **submucous fibrosis**.`],[`Key history`,`**ASK every patient at every visit** (include smokeless); type, amount, pack-years; **time to first use and number per day**; readiness in next 30 days; past attempts and why they failed; triggers; alcohol, mental illness; contraindications; CYP1A2 drugs.`],[`Key examination`,`Stained fingers, clubbing, BP, BMI, waist, peripheral pulses; **oral cavity in every smokeless user - leukoplakia, erythroplakia, ulcer, submucous fibrosis (mouth opening under 3 fingers)**; neck nodes; chest signs.`],[`Investigations`,`No test for diagnosis; **exhaled CO over about 10 ppm** confirms smoking and tracks abstinence; spirometry over 35-40 with symptoms (**FEV1/FVC under 0.7**); CXR, CBNAAT for cough over 2 weeks; **biopsy oral lesion over 2-3 weeks**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**5 A's** (Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange); quit date within 2 weeks, total abstinence; **combination NRT (21 mg patch + 2/4 mg gum or lozenge)** or **varenicline 0.5 mg OD d1-3, 0.5 mg BD d4-7, then 1 mg BD for 12 weeks (+12)**; behavioural support doubles benefit.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Bupropion SR 150 mg OD x 3 days -> 150 mg BD, 7-12 weeks**, start 1-2 weeks before quit date; **cytisine 1.5 mg 25-day taper (WHO 2024 recommended, cheap)**; combinations (varenicline + NRT); nortriptyline second line; **e-cigarettes banned in India (PECA 2019)**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Patch**: clean hairless skin above waist, press 10 s, rotate daily, remove at night if dreams; **gum: chew to tingle and park**, 8-12/day, no tea, coffee or cola 15 min before; **lozenge**: dissolve 20-30 min, do not chew; 4 mg if first use within 30 minutes.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: behavioural first, then intermittent NRT** (gum, lozenge or 16-h patch); no varenicline or bupropion; **stable CVD: NRT safe** (caution within 2 weeks of MI); **eGFR under 30: varenicline max 0.5 mg BD**; adolescents NRT from 12 years.`],[`Complications`,`Weight gain 4-5 kg, withdrawal, **bupropion seizures (1 in 1000)**, varenicline nausea (30%), neuropsychiatric events (EAGLES - no excess), **rising clozapine, olanzapine, theophylline levels on quitting**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Oral ulcer over 3 weeks, red or speckled patch, induration, trismus -> urgent biopsy; haemoptysis, weight loss -> TB and lung cancer work-up; mood change or suicidal thoughts on drugs; seizure on bupropion; repeated failures -> TCC.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Week 1, week 2, 1 month, monthly for 3 months**; CO reading, withdrawal, NRT technique and dose, side effects, mood, weight; **reduce clozapine/olanzapine 25-30%**, check INR; lapse = information, not failure.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NTCP**: Tobacco Cessation Centres, **Quitline 1800-11-2356**, **mCessation 011-22901701**; **COTPA 2003** - s.4 public smoking, s.5 advertising, **s.6 no sale to under-18s or within 100 yards of schools**, **s.7 85% pictorial warnings**; WHO FCTC.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2024 adult cessation guideline**: varenicline, NRT, bupropion and **cytisine** all recommended, with behavioural support and digital (mCessation) interventions.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Two questions decide the drug: **time to first use and number per day**; brief advice under 3 minutes adds 2-3% quits; drug + support gives **25-30% abstinence at a year**; NRT gum chewed like ordinary gum gives hiccups, not nicotine.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Every tobacco user - 5 A's in one consultation`,steps:[{label:`ASK - smoked and smokeless, every visit`,detail:`Record as a vital sign; type, amount, pack-years`},{label:`ADVISE - one clear, personal sentence`,detail:`Link to his own cough, BP, diabetes or child's asthma`},{label:`ASSESS - willing to quit in 30 days?`,detail:`No = 5 R's (Relevance, Risks, Rewards, Roadblocks, Repetition)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`ASSIST - quit date within 2 weeks plus drug`,detail:`First use within 30 min or 10+ a day = pharmacotherapy; Quitline 1800-11-2356`},{label:`ARRANGE - week 1, week 2, month 1, monthly to 3`,detail:`Most relapses in the first 2 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Cessation pharmacotherapy ladder`,steps:[{label:`Behavioural support for every user`,detail:`Quit date, triggers, 4 Ds; alone only if FTND 0-4, under 10/day, first use after 30 min`},{label:`First line A: combination NRT`,detail:`Patch 21 mg x 6 wk, 14 mg x 2, 7 mg x 2 + gum or lozenge 2/4 mg every 1-2 h`},{label:`First line B (equal): varenicline`,detail:`0.5 mg OD d1-3, 0.5 mg BD d4-7, 1 mg BD to 12 weeks (+12 if abstinent)`},{label:`Bupropion SR or cytisine`,detail:`Bupropion 150 mg OD x 3 d then BD; cytisine 1.5 mg 25-day taper`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Combination drugs, TCC referral`,detail:`Varenicline + NRT; severe mental illness, pregnancy failures, repeated relapse`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"psychiatry-insomnia-sleep":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Insomnia** is difficulty falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking early, with **daytime impairment**.`,`**Chronic insomnia** occurs **3 nights a week for 3 months**; shorter is short-term insomnia.`,`It occurs **despite adequate opportunity** to sleep; a night-shift worker is sleep-deprived instead.`,`A person who sleeps 5 hours and functions well is a **short sleeper**, not a patient.`,`Chronic insomnia affects **10-15% of adults**, more often women, the elderly and shift workers.`,`Insomnia usually coexists with other illness and is treated in its own right.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`ICSD-3-TR has seven groups, including insomnia, **sleep apnoea**, narcolepsy and circadian disorders.`,`Parasomnias (such as **REM behaviour disorder**) and movement disorders (**restless legs**) complete the list.`,`Grade severity with the **Insomnia Severity Index (0-28)**; treat from **15**.`,`OSA severity by AHI: **5-14 mild, 15-29 moderate, 30 or more severe**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Adenosine** builds up during waking and creates sleep pressure (Process S).`,`The **suprachiasmatic nucleus** sets the clock and triggers melatonin in darkness (Process C).`,`Caffeine blocks adenosine receptors, and **naps** discharge sleep pressure.`,`Insomniacs show **24-hour hyperarousal**: higher cortisol, heart rate and metabolic rate.`,`Lying awake in bed links the bed with alertness: **conditioned arousal**.`,`Long time in bed, naps and clock-watching **perpetuate** insomnia (Spielman 3P model).`,`Alcohol speeds sleep onset but **fragments the second half** of the night.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask whether the problem is **falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking early**.`,`Ask for a **2-week sleep diary**, as patients overestimate time awake.`,`**Always ask the bed partner** about snoring, apnoeas, kicking legs and dream enactment.`,`Early waking suggests **depression**; a racing mind at sleep onset suggests anxiety.`,`**Reduced need for sleep** with high energy is mania, not insomnia.`,`Ask about caffeine, alcohol, tobacco and drugs such as **steroids, SSRIs and beta-blockers**.`,`Ask about medical causes: pain, reflux, **orthopnoea**, nocturia and hot flushes.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check **BMI and neck circumference**; over 40 cm points to sleep apnoea.`,`Look at the airway for retrognathia, large tonsils and a crowded oropharynx.`,`Check BP and rhythm, as resistant hypertension and AF suggest OSA.`,`Score **STOP-BANG**: 5-8 is high risk for OSA and needs a sleep study.`,`**Epworth score over 10** means excessive daytime sleepiness.`,`Look for parkinsonism in REM behaviour disorder and pallor in restless legs.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Insomnia is a **clinical diagnosis**; polysomnography is not needed routinely.`,`The sleep diary gives **sleep efficiency**: total sleep time divided by time in bed.`,`Check TSH, CBC and glucose only when the history points to them.`,`In restless legs, check **ferritin**; give iron if it is **75 ng/mL or less**.`,`Order a **sleep study** when STOP-BANG is 5 or more or OSA is suspected.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**CBT-I is first line** and beats hypnotics at 6-12 months.`,`**Stimulus control**: bed only for sleep and sex; get up if not asleep in **20 minutes**.`,`Keep a **fixed rising time** and take **no daytime naps**.`,`**Sleep restriction**: time in bed equals diary sleep time, but **never under 5 hours**.`,`Add **15-20 minutes** when sleep efficiency stays above **85-90% for a week**.`,`Do not use sleep restriction in bipolar disorder, epilepsy, untreated OSA or professional drivers.`,`**Sleep hygiene** is necessary but is **not a treatment on its own**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Hypnotics are **second line**, always alongside CBT-I.`,`Limit any hypnotic to **2-4 weeks**, used intermittently, with a written stop date.`,`**Zolpidem** 5 mg (women, elderly) to 10 mg (men), taken immediately at bedtime.`,`Stop Z-drugs permanently for **sleep-walking or sleep-driving**.`,`Avoid benzodiazepines, sedating antihistamines and quetiapine as hypnotics.`,`**OSA**: weight loss, no sedatives, and **CPAP** for AHI 15 or more.`,`**Restless legs**: iron, then **gabapentin or pregabalin**; avoid dopamine agonists as standard.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Elderly**: CBT-I, **doxepin 3 mg** or melatonin; benzodiazepines are on the Beers list.`,`**Pregnancy**: CBT-I and sleep hygiene; avoid hypnotics where possible.`,`**Children**: behavioural measures first; a snoring child with mouth breathing needs ENT referral.`,`**Drivers** with excessive sleepiness must not drive until treated.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review every 1-2 weeks during CBT-I and adjust time in bed from the diary.`,`Repeat the **ISI at 4-8 weeks**; under 8 means remission.`,`Review any hypnotic at **2-4 weeks** to stop it.`,`Taper long-term benzodiazepines by **10-25% every 2-4 weeks**; never stop abruptly.`,`Refer suspected OSA, narcolepsy, **REM behaviour disorder** and refractory insomnia.`,`REM behaviour disorder in a man over 50 can precede **Parkinson disease**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Sleep drugs - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Zolpidem`,`5 mg (women, elderly) to 10 mg (men) at bedtime`,`2-4 weeks, intermittent`,`Amnesia, complex sleep behaviours, dependence`],[`Zopiclone`,`3.75 mg (elderly) to 7.5 mg at bedtime`,`2-4 weeks`,`Metallic taste, next-day sedation`],[`Doxepin (low dose)`,`3-6 mg at night`,`For sleep maintenance`,`Safe in elderly; not within 3 hours of a meal`],[`Melatonin PR`,`2 mg 1-2 hours before bed`,`Up to 13 weeks (over 55)`,`Headache; best in jet lag and shift work`],[`Mirtazapine`,`7.5-15 mg at night`,`When depression coexists`,`Weight gain, sedation`],[`Trazodone`,`25-100 mg at night`,`Off-label, with depression`,`Postural hypotension, rare priapism`],[`Pregabalin (restless legs)`,`75 mg, up to 300 mg, before bed`,`Long term`,`Dizziness, misuse; give iron first`],[`Gabapentin (restless legs)`,`300 mg, up to 1200-1800 mg, before bed`,`Long term`,`Sedation; reduce in renal impairment`]]},{heading:`Sleep disorders - clues and first treatment`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Key clue`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Chronic insomnia`,`3 nights a week for 3 months, daytime impairment`,`CBT-I`],[`Obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Snoring, witnessed apnoeas, thick neck, sleepiness`,`Weight loss, no sedatives, CPAP`],[`Restless legs syndrome`,`Evening urge to move legs, relieved by moving`,`Iron if ferritin 75 or less; gabapentin`],[`Circadian rhythm disorder`,`Cannot sleep before 2 a.m.; fine on holidays`,`Fixed wake time, morning light, melatonin`],[`Narcolepsy`,`Sleep attacks, cataplexy with laughter`,`Refer for MSLT; scheduled naps`],[`REM behaviour disorder`,`Acting out dreams, man over 50`,`Bedroom safety; melatonin; refer`]]},{heading:`Insomnia Severity Index and the action`,columns:[`ISI`,`Category`,`Action`],rows:[[`0-7`,`No clinical insomnia`,`Reassure; sleep hygiene`],[`8-14`,`Subthreshold`,`Sleep hygiene, stimulus control, diary`],[`15-21`,`Moderate`,`Full CBT-I; short hypnotic only if needed`],[`22-28`,`Severe`,`CBT-I; screen for depression and OSA; refer if refractory`]]}],remember:[`Chronic insomnia: **3 nights a week for 3 months** with daytime impairment.`,`**CBT-I first**; sleep hygiene alone is not a treatment.`,`The **20-minute rule**, a fixed rising time and no naps.`,`Ask the bed partner: snoring, kicking legs and dream enactment are invisible to the patient.`,`Never give a hypnotic in untreated sleep apnoea.`,`**STOP-BANG**: Snoring, Tired, Observed apnoea, Pressure, BMI over 35, Age over 50, Neck over 40 cm, Gender male.`,`Restless legs: ferritin 75 or less means iron; gabapentin or pregabalin before dopamine agonists.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AASM 2024 RLS guideline: alpha2-delta ligands (gabapentin, pregabalin) strong first line; conditional recommendation against standard use of dopamine agonists (augmentation); iron if ferritin 75 ng/mL or less, IV ferric carboxymaltose strong recommendation.`,`FDA December 2024: tirzepatide approved for moderate to severe OSA with obesity, as an adjunct to CPAP and lifestyle.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - insomnia and sleep disorders`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep or early waking, 3 nights a week for 3 months, despite adequate opportunity, with daytime impairment**; under 3 months = short-term insomnia; a short sleeper without impairment is not a patient.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**ICSD-3-TR 7 groups**: insomnia, sleep-related breathing (OSA), central hypersomnolence (narcolepsy), circadian rhythm, parasomnias (RBD), movement (RLS), other; insomnia subtypes - **chronic (3 months+) vs short-term**; comorbid insomnia is the rule.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**ISI 0-7 none, 8-14 subthreshold, 15-21 moderate, 22-28 severe** (treat from 15); **sleep efficiency = TST / time in bed** (normal over 85%); **RLS = 5 IRLSSG criteria** (urge, worse at rest, relieved by moving, evening, no mimic); **narcolepsy = MSLT**; RBD = PSG.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Spielman 3P**: predisposing (anxious, female, age), precipitating (stress, illness, shifts), **perpetuating (long time in bed, naps, clock-watching, alcohol, hypnotics)**; comorbid depression, anxiety, pain, COPD, HF, nocturia, menopause; drugs - steroids, SSRIs, beta-blockers, diuretics.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**24-hour hyperarousal** (cortisol, beta EEG) + **conditioned arousal in bed** -> insomnia; sleep = **Process S (adenosine)** + **Process C (SCN, melatonin)**; VLPO GABA flip-flop vs orexin, histamine, NA arousal - each hypnotic acts on one limb.`],[`Key history`,`24-hour schedule, **2-week sleep diary**, daytime impairment vs true sleepiness; **bed partner**: snoring, apnoeas (OSA), kicking (PLMD), dream enactment (RBD); **RLS 4 features**; PHQ-9, GAD-7, **mania** (reduced need for sleep); alcohol, caffeine, hypnotics; beliefs.`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, **neck over 40 cm**, Mallampati 3-4, retrognathia, BP, AF, thyroid, heart failure, pallor (RLS), parkinsonism (RBD); **STOP-BANG 0-2 low, 3-4 intermediate, 5-8 high**; **Epworth over 10 = excessive sleepiness**.`],[`Investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis from diary (**sleep efficiency = TST / time in bed**); TSH, CBC, glucose if indicated; **ferritin and TSAT in RLS**; **PSG or home sleep test for STOP-BANG 5+** (AHI 5-14 mild, 15-29 moderate, 30+ severe); MSLT for narcolepsy; **no routine PSG for insomnia**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**CBT-I first line** (4-8 sessions): **stimulus control** (bed only when sleepy, 20-minute rule, fixed rising time, no naps), **sleep restriction** (time in bed = diary sleep time, never under 5 h; add 15-20 min when efficiency over 85-90%), cognitive therapy, relaxation; sleep hygiene alone is not treatment.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Hypnotic **2-4 weeks, intermittent, written stop date**: **zolpidem 5 mg (women, elderly) - 10 mg**, **zopiclone 3.75-7.5 mg**, **doxepin 3-6 mg** (maintenance), **melatonin PR 2 mg over 55**, daridorexant 25-50 mg; mirtazapine 7.5-15 mg or trazodone 25-100 mg with depression.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**CPAP for AHI 15+, or 5-14 with symptoms or CV disease**, at least **4 h on 70% of nights**; mandibular advancement for mild OSA; **tirzepatide (FDA 2024) for moderate-severe OSA with obesity** as adjunct; **RLS: iron if ferritin 75 or less, then gabapentin 300 mg or pregabalin 75 mg**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Elderly: CBT-I, doxepin 3 mg or melatonin; avoid benzodiazepines and antihistamines (Beers)**; pregnancy: CBT-I, avoid hypnotics; children: routine, graduated extinction, melatonin 1-3 mg in neurodevelopmental disorders; **no sleep restriction in bipolar, epilepsy, untreated OSA, drivers**.`],[`Complications`,`Z-drug **complex sleep behaviours** (stop permanently), falls, road accidents, dependence and **rebound insomnia**; untreated OSA -> hypertension, AF, crashes; **RLS augmentation on dopamine agonists**; RBD -> later Parkinson or Lewy body disease.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Snoring with witnessed apnoeas (**never a hypnotic**), sleepy driver (Epworth over 15), **mania**, early waking with depression and suicide risk, dream enactment over 50, cataplexy, refractory insomnia after CBT-I, hypnotic dependence, snoring child.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`CBT-I review **every 1-2 weeks** with diary; **ISI at 4-8 weeks** (fall of 8 or under 8 = response/remission); hypnotic stop at 2-4 weeks; **ferritin at 3 months** in RLS; CPAP adherence, BP, Epworth; taper long-term hypnotics **10-25% every 2-4 weeks** via diazepam.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AASM 2024 RLS: dopamine agonists no longer standard (augmentation); gabapentinoids and iron (IV ferric carboxymaltose) strongly recommended**; tirzepatide approved for OSA with obesity (FDA 2024).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**CBT-I outlasts any drug**; ask the bed partner; never a hypnotic in untreated OSA; Z-drug sleep-walking = stop for good; doxepin 3-6 mg and melatonin are safest in the elderly; check ferritin in every RLS.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Chronic insomnia - assessment to treatment`,steps:[{label:`3 nights/week for 3 months with daytime impairment?`,detail:`No impairment = short sleeper; under 3 months = short-term`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Bed partner: snoring, apnoeas, kicks, dream enactment`,detail:`STOP-BANG 5+ = sleep study; never a hypnotic`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Screen mood, mania, alcohol, drugs, medical causes`,detail:`PHQ-9, GAD-7; move SSRI and diuretic to morning`},{label:`2-week sleep diary and ISI`,detail:`Sleep efficiency; treat from ISI 15`},{label:`CBT-I: stimulus control plus sleep restriction`,detail:`20-minute rule, fixed rising time, no naps; extend at 85-90%`},{label:`Short intermittent hypnotic only if needed`,detail:`Zolpidem 5-10 mg or zopiclone 3.75-7.5 mg, 2-4 weeks, stop date`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Restless legs syndrome - AASM 2024 ladder`,caption:`Dopamine agonists are no longer standard treatment because of augmentation.`,steps:[{label:`Remove aggravators`,detail:`Antihistamines, metoclopramide, SSRIs, TCAs, antipsychotics, caffeine, alcohol`},{label:`Iron if ferritin 75 or less or TSAT under 20%`,detail:`Ferrous sulfate 200 mg OD or alternate days x 3 months; recheck ferritin`},{label:`IV ferric carboxymaltose (strong)`,detail:`Ferritin 75-100 ng/mL (IV only), or oral iron fails or is not tolerated`},{label:`Alpha2-delta ligand (strong)`,detail:`Gabapentin 300 mg (to 1200-1800 mg) or pregabalin 75 mg (to 300 mg) 1-2 h before bed`,tone:`good`},{label:`Dopamine agonist only short term or specialist`,detail:`Pramipexole 0.125-0.5 mg; watch augmentation and impulse control`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Hypnotics - which and for whom`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose`,`Best for`,`Key caution`],rows:[[`Zolpidem`,`5 mg women/elderly, 10 mg men`,`Sleep onset, short term`,`Complex sleep behaviours, next-day impairment`],[`Zopiclone`,`3.75-7.5 mg`,`Onset and maintenance`,`Metallic taste, dependence`],[`Doxepin low dose`,`3-6 mg`,`Sleep maintenance, elderly`,`Not within 3 h of a meal`],[`Melatonin PR`,`2 mg 1-2 h before bed, up to 13 weeks`,`Over 55, jet lag, circadian disorders`,`Small effect in ordinary insomnia`],[`Benzodiazepines`,`Avoid`,`Not for chronic insomnia`,`Falls, hip fracture, dependence (Beers)`]]}]},"psychiatry-psychosis-schizophrenia":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Psychosis** is loss of contact with reality: delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech and behaviour.`,`**Schizophrenia** needs **2 of 5 domains for 1 month**, with **6 months** of total disturbance.`,`At least one domain must be **delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech**.`,`**Negative symptoms** (blunted affect, alogia, avolition) cause most disability and respond least to drugs.`,`Lifetime prevalence is about **1%**; onset is late teens in men, about 5 years later in women.`,`In India the treatment gap for psychosis is about **75%**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Brief psychotic disorder** lasts 1 day to under 1 month, with full recovery.`,`**Schizophreniform disorder** lasts 1 to under 6 months.`,`**Schizophrenia** lasts 6 months or more.`,`**Delusional disorder** is delusions alone for at least 1 month.`,`**Schizoaffective disorder** adds 2 weeks of psychosis without mood symptoms.`,`ICD-11 needs only 1 month and has **dropped the old subtypes** like paranoid and hebephrenic.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Excess **mesolimbic dopamine** gives ordinary events false importance, causing delusions and hallucinations.`,`Low **mesocortical dopamine** causes negative and cognitive symptoms.`,`**NMDA glutamate hypofunction** drives the dopamine excess; ketamine reproduces the illness.`,`Blocking **nigrostriatal** D2 receptors causes extrapyramidal side effects.`,`Blocking **tuberoinfundibular** D2 receptors raises prolactin.`,`Treatment needs **65-70% D2 occupancy**; above 80% causes EPS.`,`Heritability is about **80%**, and heavy adolescent **cannabis** use raises risk.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Interview the patient and family separately**; the family dates the decline.`,`Ask about voices without arguing; **command hallucinations** raise risk.`,`Ask about mood, because psychotic mania and psychotic depression are treated differently.`,`Take a substance history: **cannabis (ganja, bhang)**, alcohol and stimulants.`,`Ask about drugs: **steroid psychosis** is the commonest iatrogenic cause.`,`Assess risk at every visit: suicide, violence, self-neglect and access to pesticide or weapons.`,`Ask about recent delivery; **postpartum psychosis** starts within days to 2 weeks.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for self-neglect and **hallucinatory behaviour**, such as muttering or smiling to self.`,`Check thought form: derailment, neologisms and **thought block**.`,`Affect is often **blunted or incongruent** in schizophrenia.`,`Orientation is **usually intact**; disorientation means **delirium** until proved otherwise.`,`Prominent **visual or tactile hallucinations** suggest delirium, drugs or organic disease.`,`Record a **metabolic baseline**: BMI, waist and BP.`,`Under 40 with tremor or dystonia, look for **Kayser-Fleischer rings** (Wilson disease).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`First-episode panel: **CBC, glucose, electrolytes, calcium, renal and liver function, TSH, B12**.`,`Add HIV, VDRL and a **urine drug screen** where risk exists.`,`Before any antipsychotic: weight, waist, BP, **fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipids and prolactin**.`,`**ECG** before haloperidol; a QTc over **500 ms** means stop the QT drug.`,`Image the brain for focal signs, seizures, confusion or a **first episode after 40**.`,`Do not delay treatment waiting for a perfect work-up.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Shorten the duration of untreated psychosis**; start treatment or refer the same week.`,`Use **one antipsychotic**, start low, and choose by side-effect profile.`,`**Risperidone 2-4 mg** is cheap and supplied free through the DMHP.`,`**Aripiprazole 10-15 mg** is weight-neutral and spares prolactin; good for young women.`,`An adequate trial is **4-6 weeks** at a therapeutic dose.`,`**Family intervention** reduces expressed emotion and halves relapse.`,`Without medication, relapse at 1 year is **60-80%**; with it, 15-25%.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Treatment resistance** is failure of two adequate trials with confirmed adherence.`,`**Clozapine** is the only drug proven better in resistant illness, and it reduces suicide.`,`Clozapine needs a **CBC weekly for 18 weeks**, then fortnightly to 1 year, then monthly.`,`Stop clozapine if neutrophils fall **below 1.5 x 10^9/L**.`,`Use **depot injections** for relapse due to non-adherence.`,`Continue **1-2 years after a first episode**, and long term after two or more.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Acute dystonia**: oculogyric crisis or torticollis; give **promethazine 25-50 mg IM**.`,`**Akathisia** looks like worse psychosis and drives suicide; reduce the dose and add propranolol.`,`**Tardive dyskinesia** is worsened by anticholinergics; stop them.`,`**NMS**: fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion and high CK; stop the drug and admit.`,`Fever or sore throat on clozapine needs an **urgent blood count**.`,`Stopping smoking can **double clozapine levels**; reduce the dose by 25-30%.`,`Weight gain, diabetes and dyslipidaemia are worst with **olanzapine and clozapine**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`For agitation, de-escalate first, then give **haloperidol 5 mg plus promethazine 25-50 mg IM**.`,`Never give IM olanzapine within 1 hour of a parenteral benzodiazepine.`,`Weigh weekly for 6 weeks; check glucose, lipids and BP at **12 weeks** and yearly.`,`**MHCA section 89**: supported admission up to **30 days** on two opinions.`,`MHCA bans **chaining and unmodified ECT**.`,`Refer every first episode, resistance, NMS, catatonia, postpartum psychosis and high risk.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antipsychotics - doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Risperidone`,`1-2 mg oral at night, up to 2-4 mg`,`1-2 years after first episode`,`Prolactin rise; EPS above 6 mg`],[`Olanzapine`,`5-10 mg oral at night, up to 20 mg`,`1-2 years after first episode`,`Worst weight gain, diabetes, lipids`],[`Aripiprazole`,`10-15 mg oral morning, up to 30 mg`,`1-2 years after first episode`,`Akathisia; weight-neutral, prolactin-sparing`],[`Quetiapine`,`25-50 mg at night, up to 300-600 mg/day`,`1-2 years after first episode`,`Sedation; least EPS, good in Parkinson disease`],[`Haloperidol`,`1.5-5 mg oral at night; 5 mg IM for agitation`,`As needed`,`Highest EPS and tardive dyskinesia; ECG first`],[`Clozapine`,`12.5 mg day 1, to 300 mg by 2-3 weeks`,`Long term, specialist`,`Agranulocytosis, myocarditis, constipation, seizures`],[`Paliperidone palmitate`,`150 mg IM day 1, 100 mg day 8, then monthly`,`Long term depot`,`EPS, prolactin rise`],[`Haloperidol decanoate`,`Test 25 mg, then 50-100 mg IM 4-weekly`,`Long term depot`,`EPS, tardive dyskinesia`]]},{heading:`Psychotic disorders by duration`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Duration`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Brief psychotic disorder`,`1 day to under 1 month`,`Full recovery; often after stress`],[`Schizophreniform disorder`,`1 to under 6 months`,`Two-thirds progress to schizophrenia`],[`Schizophrenia`,`6 months or more`,`2 of 5 domains with functional decline`],[`Delusional disorder`,`1 month or more`,`Delusions only; function preserved`],[`Schizoaffective disorder`,`Mood episodes plus 2 weeks psychosis alone`,`Mood symptoms for most of the illness`]]},{heading:`Antipsychotic movement disorders`,columns:[`Type`,`Onset`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute dystonia`,`Hours to days`,`Promethazine IM or trihexyphenidyl 2 mg`],[`Akathisia`,`Days to weeks`,`Reduce dose; propranolol`],[`Parkinsonism`,`Weeks`,`Reduce dose or switch; trihexyphenidyl short term`],[`Tardive dyskinesia`,`Months to years`,`Stop anticholinergics; switch to clozapine`],[`Neuroleptic malignant syndrome`,`24-72 hours`,`Stop drug, ICU, cool, fluids, lorazepam`]]}],remember:[`Schizophrenia: **2 of 5 domains for 1 month**, 6 months in all.`,`Under 1 month is brief psychotic disorder; 1-6 months is schizophreniform.`,`Dopamine pathways: mesolimbic treats, nigrostriatal gives EPS, tuberoinfundibular raises prolactin.`,`Disorientation means delirium, not schizophrenia.`,`**Clozapine after two failed trials**; CBC weekly for 18 weeks.`,`Anticholinergics help dystonia but worsen tardive dyskinesia.`,`TREC-India: **haloperidol plus promethazine IM** for agitation.`,`MHCA 2017: supported admission up to 30 days; chaining is banned.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`FDA September 2024: xanomeline-trospium (M1/M4 muscarinic agonist) approved - first schizophrenia drug without D2 blockade; not yet available in India.`,`FDA June 2025: clozapine REMS removed in the US; ANC monitoring per label continues - Indian CBC schedule unchanged.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - psychosis and schizophrenia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Psychosis = loss of reality testing** - delusions (fixed false beliefs out of keeping with culture), hallucinations (perception without stimulus), disorganised speech or behaviour; **schizophrenia** is its commonest chronic form, with negative and cognitive symptoms driving disability.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By duration: **brief psychotic (1 day-1 month)**, **schizophreniform (1-6 months)**, **schizophrenia (6 months+)**, delusional disorder (1 month, delusions only), schizoaffective; **ICD-11: 1 month suffices, subtypes dropped**, ATPD; Schneider first-rank symptoms not required.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**DSM-5-TR schizophrenia: 2 of 5** (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, disorganised/catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms) **for 1 month, one of the first three**, + **6 months of disturbance** + functional decline; exclude mood disorder, substance, medical cause; **ICD-11 needs only 1 month**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Heritability **about 80%** (first-degree 10%, both parents 40%, MZ twin 45-50%), obstetric complications, urban birth, migration, **adolescent cannabis (2-4 times risk)**, 22q11.2 deletion; prevalence **about 1%**; onset late teens (men), 5 years later (women).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`NMDA hypofunction -> glutamate disinhibition -> **mesolimbic dopamine excess (aberrant salience) -> positive symptoms**; **mesocortical hypodopaminergia -> negative and cognitive**; D2 occupancy **65-70% therapeutic, over 72% prolactin, over 80% EPS**.`],[`Key history`,`Patient alone and family separately; voices (commands), delusions by name, onset and **duration of untreated psychosis**, mood symptoms, **cannabis and drugs (steroids, levodopa, isoniazid, efavirenz)**, medical red flags, **risk (suicide, violence, self-neglect)**, adherence, traditional healers, recent delivery.`],[`Key examination`,`Hallucinatory behaviour, catatonia, formal thought disorder, blunted or incongruent affect, poor insight; **cognition and orientation intact** (else delirium); BMI, waist, BP; focal signs, meningism; **Kayser-Fleischer rings** under 40; EPS at every visit.`],[`Investigations`,`CBC, glucose, electrolytes, calcium, RFT, LFT, **TSH, B12**, HIV, VDRL, urine drug screen; **metabolic baseline: weight, waist, BP, HbA1c/FBS, lipids, prolactin**; **ECG (QTc over 450/470; over 500 stop)**; CT/MRI for focal signs, seizures or first episode over 40.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**One antipsychotic, start low**: **risperidone 1-2 -> 2-4 mg** (max 6-8), **olanzapine 5-10 mg** (max 20), **aripiprazole 10-15 mg** (max 30), quetiapine 300-600 mg; **adequate trial 4-6 weeks**; family psychoeducation, CBTp, early warning signs.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Two failed adequate trials -> clozapine** (12.5 mg day 1, up 25-50 mg/day to 300 mg by 2-3 weeks; usual 200-450 mg; level 350 mcg/L) - reduces suicide; non-adherence -> **depots: haloperidol decanoate 50-100 mg 4-weekly, paliperidone palmitate 150 mg d1, 100 mg d8, then 75 mg monthly**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Rapid tranquillisation**: de-escalate -> oral olanzapine 10 mg or lorazepam 1-2 mg -> **IM haloperidol 5 mg + promethazine 25-50 mg (TREC)** or lorazepam 1-2 mg IM; obs every 15 min for 1 hour; **no IM olanzapine within 1 h of parenteral benzodiazepine**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: continue effective drug (olanzapine, quetiapine, haloperidol), avoid valproate; **elderly/dementia**: stroke and mortality risk - lowest dose; **young women/obese**: aripiprazole; **Parkinson/Lewy**: quetiapine; renal: halve risperidone, reduce amisulpride.`],[`Complications`,`**Acute dystonia** (promethazine 25-50 mg IM, trihexyphenidyl 2 mg), **akathisia** (propranolol 10-40 mg), parkinsonism, **tardive dyskinesia** (stop anticholinergics, VMAT2), **NMS** (stop, cool, fluids, lorazepam, bromocriptine/dantrolene), hyperprolactinaemia, **metabolic syndrome (metformin 500 mg BD)**, QTc.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Delirium features, fever + rigidity (**NMS**), command voices or homicidal intent, catatonia, **first episode over 40 or with neuro signs**, postpartum psychosis, **clozapine fever or sore throat (agranulocytosis) or chest pain (myocarditis)**, Wilson features.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Weight **weekly x 6 weeks, 12 weeks, 1 year, annually**; glucose and lipids **baseline, 12 weeks, annually**; prolactin baseline and 6 months; **clozapine CBC weekly x 18 weeks, fortnightly to 1 year, then monthly; stop if neutrophils under 1.5**; maintenance **1-2 years after first episode**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**MHCA 2017**: nominated representative, advance directive, **supported admission s.89 up to 30 days (two opinions), s.90 beyond 30 days (two psychiatrists + Board)**, **s.94 emergency 72 h**, chaining and unmodified ECT banned, **s.21(4) insurance parity**; DMHP free drugs and depots; RPwD disability certificate; Tele-MANAS 14416.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Xanomeline-trospium (FDA 2024)** - first non-D2 antipsychotic (M1/M4 agonist), no EPS or prolactin rise; **clozapine REMS removed in the US (FDA 2025)** but ANC monitoring continues; NICE CG178 core recommendations unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Shorten the **duration of untreated psychosis**; akathisia mimics worsening psychosis and drives suicide; anticholinergics worsen tardive dyskinesia; **stopping smoking raises clozapine levels - cut dose 25-30%**; relapse 60-80% at 1 year off drugs vs 15-25% on them.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`First-episode psychosis in OPD`,steps:[{label:`Delusions, hallucinations or disorganisation`,detail:`Interview patient alone and family separately`},{label:`Disoriented, fluctuating, febrile or focal signs?`,detail:`Yes = delirium or organic cause - investigate first`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Command voices, suicidal/homicidal intent, catatonia`,detail:`Same-day admission; MHCA supported admission if needed`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Bloods, drug screen, metabolic baseline, ECG`,detail:`TSH, B12, HIV, VDRL; weight, waist, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin`},{label:`One antipsychotic at low dose, 4-6 week trial`,detail:`Risperidone 2-4 mg, aripiprazole 10-15 mg or olanzapine 10 mg`},{label:`Family psychoeducation, relapse plan, DMHP link`,detail:`Continue 1-2 years after first episode`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Schizophrenia drug escalation`,steps:[{label:`First antipsychotic, adequate trial`,detail:`4-6 weeks at therapeutic dose; check adherence`},{label:`Switch to a second antipsychotic`,detail:`Different profile; one ideally atypical`},{label:`Non-adherence: long-acting injectable`,detail:`Haloperidol decanoate 50-100 mg 4-weekly; paliperidone 75 mg monthly`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Treatment resistance: clozapine`,detail:`After two failed trials; 300 mg by 2-3 weeks; CBC weekly x 18 weeks`,tone:`good`},{label:`Clozapine hazards`,detail:`Neutrophils under 1.5 stop; myocarditis first 2 months; laxative for ileus`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Antipsychotic movement disorders - fast recall`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Onset`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute dystonia`,`Hours-days`,`Promethazine 25-50 mg IM or trihexyphenidyl 2 mg`],[`Akathisia`,`Days-weeks`,`Reduce dose; propranolol 10-40 mg BD-TDS`],[`Parkinsonism`,`Weeks`,`Reduce or switch; trihexyphenidyl 2 mg; never levodopa`],[`Tardive dyskinesia`,`Months-years`,`Stop anticholinergics; switch to clozapine; VMAT2 inhibitor`],[`NMS`,`24-72 hours`,`Stop drug, ICU, cool, IV fluids, lorazepam, bromocriptine or dantrolene`]]}]},"psychiatry-delirium-dementia":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Delirium** is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of **attention and awareness** over hours to days.`,`Delirium is a **medical emergency** caused by illness, drugs or withdrawal.`,`**Dementia** is insidious, progressive cognitive decline that **impairs independence**, with clear consciousness.`,`**Mild cognitive impairment** leaves independence intact; 10-15% a year progress to dementia.`,`Delirium affects 20-30% of older inpatients and **up to 50% after hip fracture surgery**.`,`Dementia affects about **7.4% of Indians aged 60 and over**.`,`**Any sudden change in a person with dementia is delirium** until proved otherwise.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Delirium subtypes: **hypoactive** (quiet, most missed, worst prognosis), hyperactive and mixed.`,`Dementia by cause: **Alzheimer about 60%**, **vascular 20-30%**, Lewy body, frontotemporal and mixed.`,`**Reversible causes**: B12 deficiency, hypothyroidism, NPH, subdural haematoma, neurosyphilis and depression.`,`Onset, attention, consciousness and course separate delirium from dementia.`,`About **45% of dementia** is linked to 14 modifiable risk factors (Lancet 2024).`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Delirium is **acetylcholine deficiency** with relative dopamine excess.`,`Infection or surgery releases cytokines that activate **primed microglia** in the ageing brain.`,`Delirium occurs when a **precipitating insult** exceeds the patient's predisposing vulnerability.`,`In Alzheimer disease, **amyloid-beta 42** forms extracellular plaques.`,`Hyperphosphorylated **tau** forms tangles, spreading from the hippocampus outward.`,`Loss of cholinergic neurons in the **nucleus basalis of Meynert** is why cholinesterase inhibitors help.`,`Lewy body dementia has **severe neuroleptic sensitivity** from nigrostriatal loss.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Collateral history is essential**: what was the patient like a week ago?`,`Onset over hours to days suggests delirium; insidious over years suggests Alzheimer disease.`,`Ask about new drugs, falls, fever, poor intake, and **constipation or urinary retention**.`,`Review all drugs, especially **anticholinergics, benzodiazepines and opioids**, and anything just stopped.`,`**Instrumental ADLs** like money and medicines fail first, then basic ADLs.`,`Visual hallucinations, parkinsonism and dream enactment suggest **Lewy body dementia**.`,`Ask about **carer strain** and elder abuse.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Test attention**: months of the year backwards; 7 or more correct is normal.`,`**4AT of 4 or more** means possible delirium; it takes 2 minutes.`,`**CAM**: acute fluctuating onset **and** inattention, **plus** disorganised thinking **or** altered consciousness.`,`In dementia use **MMSE, Hindi MSE or MoCA**, correcting for education.`,`Check **capillary glucose and SpO2 immediately**.`,`Palpate the bladder and do a **rectal examination** for retention and impaction.`,`Look for parkinsonism (Lewy body) and **gait apraxia** (NPH).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Delirium: CBC, renal function, **sodium, calcium**, LFT, CRP, urine, ECG and chest X-ray.`,`In India, add **malaria, dengue and typhoid** tests where relevant.`,`**Medication reconciliation** is itself an investigation.`,`Dementia screen: **TSH, B12, folate, calcium**, glucose, renal and liver function.`,`**CT or MRI once** in every new dementia, to exclude subdural haematoma, tumour and NPH.`,`**ECG** before a cholinesterase inhibitor if pulse is under 60 or there is syncope.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`In delirium, **treating the cause is the treatment**; use **PINCH ME**.`,`Reorient with a clock, calendar and a **familiar relative** at the bedside.`,`Restore **spectacles and hearing aids**; keep nights dark and quiet.`,`Avoid restraints and catheters; mobilise early.`,`In dementia, manage **BPSD without drugs first**: look for pain, constipation and retention.`,`**Donepezil 5 mg at night** for 4-6 weeks, then 10 mg, for mild to moderate Alzheimer disease.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Antipsychotics do not treat delirium**; use them only for danger or severe distress.`,`**Haloperidol 0.25-0.5 mg**, with a maximum of 2-3 mg in 24 hours in the elderly.`,`**Benzodiazepines worsen delirium**, except in alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal.`,`Add **memantine** (up to 20 mg) in moderate to severe Alzheimer disease.`,`**Vascular dementia** has no cognitive drug; control BP, diabetes, lipids and AF.`,`In BPSD, antipsychotics raise **stroke and death risk**; use risperidone briefly, then taper.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Lewy body or Parkinson disease**: never haloperidol or risperidone; use quetiapine.`,`Lewy body dementia responds well to **cholinesterase inhibitors**.`,`**Frontotemporal dementia**: cholinesterase inhibitors are not recommended; SSRIs or trazodone may help.`,`**Alcohol withdrawal delirium**: benzodiazepine, with **thiamine before glucose**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Reassess cognition **4-8 weeks after delirium**; it often unmasks dementia.`,`Review dementia **every 6 months**: cognition, function, pulse, falls and carer strain.`,`Review antipsychotics every **6-12 weeks** and taper to stop.`,`Refer young-onset (under 65), rapidly progressive, Lewy body or frontotemporal dementia, and NPH.`,`Support the carer through **ARDSI**, respite and RPwD disability certification.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in delirium and dementia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Haloperidol (delirium)`,`0.25-0.5 mg oral or IM; max 2-3 mg/24 h`,`Days; review daily`,`EPS, QTc; never in Lewy body dementia`],[`Quetiapine`,`12.5-25 mg once or twice daily`,`Shortest time`,`Sedation, falls; choice in Parkinson disease`],[`Donepezil`,`5 mg at night, then 10 mg after 4-6 weeks`,`While benefit continues`,`Nausea, bradycardia, vivid dreams`],[`Rivastigmine`,`1.5 mg BD to 3-6 mg BD, or patch 4.6-9.5 mg/24 h`,`While benefit continues`,`GI upset (less with patch), bradycardia`],[`Galantamine`,`PR 8 mg OD, then 16 mg (max 24 mg)`,`While benefit continues`,`Nausea, bradycardia`],[`Memantine`,`5 mg OD, up 5 mg weekly to 20 mg`,`Moderate to severe disease`,`Dizziness, headache; 10 mg if CrCl 5-29`],[`Risperidone (BPSD)`,`0.25 mg BD, up to 1 mg BD`,`Up to 6 weeks`,`Stroke and death risk; avoid in Lewy body`],[`Lorazepam`,`0.5-1 mg 6-8 hourly, tapering`,`Withdrawal delirium only`,`Worsens other delirium; thiamine first`]]},{heading:`Delirium versus dementia versus depression`,columns:[`Feature`,`Delirium`,`Dementia`,`Depression`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Hours to days`,`Months to years`,`Weeks, often after a loss`],[`Course in the day`,`Fluctuates, worse at night`,`Stable`,`Worse in the morning`],[`Attention`,`Impaired, the key sign`,`Normal until late`,`Distractible but testable`],[`Consciousness`,`Altered`,`Clear`,`Clear`],[`Hallucinations`,`Common, visual`,`Uncommon, except Lewy body`,`Rare`],[`Answers`,`Rambling, inconsistent`,`Near-miss, tries hard`,`'I don't know', poor effort`],[`Reversibility`,`Reversible; find the cause`,`Progressive`,`Reversible with treatment`]]},{heading:`4AT score for delirium`,columns:[`Item`,`Abnormal finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`Alertness`,`Clearly abnormal`,`4`],[`AMT4 (age, birth date, place, year)`,`2 or more mistakes`,`2`],[`Attention (months backwards)`,`Under 7 correct or refuses`,`1`],[`Acute change or fluctuation`,`Yes, in past 2 weeks`,`4`],[`Total`,`4 or more possible delirium; 1-3 possible cognitive impairment`,`0-12`]]}],remember:[`Delirium is **yes, yes, yes**: sudden onset, impaired attention, altered consciousness.`,`**PINCH ME**: Pain, Infection, Nutrition, Constipation, Hydration, Medication, Environment.`,`Hypoactive delirium is the commonest, most missed and most deadly form.`,`Never haloperidol or risperidone in Lewy body dementia or Parkinson disease.`,`Benzodiazepines only for withdrawal delirium.`,`Cholinesterase inhibitors are vagotonic: check the pulse.`,`Define dementia by **loss of independence**, not by the test score.`,`Reassess cognition 4-8 weeks after a delirium resolves.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NICE 2025 (final draft, under reconsideration after 2026 appeals): lecanemab and donanemab not recommended for NHS use - benefit about 4-6 months' delay, high cost, ARIA and MRI burden.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - delirium versus dementia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Delirium**: acute (hours-days), **fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness** + one cognitive deficit, caused by illness, drug or withdrawal; **dementia (major NCD)**: insidious decline in 1 or more of 6 domains **impairing independence**, clear consciousness.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Delirium: **hypoactive (commonest, most missed), hyperactive, mixed**; dementia by cause: **Alzheimer about 60%, vascular 20-30%, Lewy body, frontotemporal, mixed**; **MCI** = decline with independence preserved (10-15%/year convert); reversible: B12, thyroid, NPH, subdural, syphilis, depression.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Delirium: **predisposing** (age over 65, dementia, frailty, sensory loss) + **precipitating** (infection, drugs, surgery, retention, dehydration) - **PINCH ME**; dementia: age, **APOE e4**, Down syndrome, vascular risk; **Lancet 2024: 14 modifiable factors, about 45%**; India **7.4% over 60** (LASI-DAD).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Delirium: **acetylcholine deficiency + dopamine excess + neuroinflammation** (IL-6, primed microglia); Alzheimer: **amyloid-beta 42 plaques + tau tangles** -> entorhinal/hippocampal loss -> **nucleus basalis cholinergic loss** + NMDA excitotoxicity; Lewy: alpha-synuclein, **neuroleptic sensitivity**.`],[`Key history`,`**Collateral history** (a week ago vs now), speed of onset, fluctuation, sundowning; **5 cause questions - new drugs, fall, infection, intake, constipation/retention**; **drug list vs Beers** (anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, opioids); IADL then ADL loss; subtype features; depression; carer strain, abuse.`],[`Key examination`,`Consciousness (**RASS**); **attention - months backwards (7+ normal)**; **4AT**; CAM; SpO2, glucose, temperature; chest, urine, **bladder scan and PR**; focal signs, parkinsonism, gait apraxia; **MMSE/HMSE or MoCA** only when not delirious; vision and hearing.`],[`Investigations`,`Delirium: **glucose, SpO2, CBC, U&E, calcium, LFT, CRP, urine, ECG, CXR**, malaria/dengue/typhoid as relevant; CT if fall, focal signs, anticoagulant; dementia: **B12, folate, TSH, calcium, glucose, VDRL/HIV**, **CT/MRI once**; ECG before cholinesterase inhibitor if bradycardic.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**4AT 4+ = possible delirium, 1-3 cognitive impairment**; **CAM = acute onset/fluctuation AND inattention + (disorganised thinking OR altered consciousness)**; MMSE **23 or less** impaired (AD: mild 21-26, moderate 10-20, severe under 10); **MoCA under 26** (add 1 if 12 years education or less).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Delirium: **treat the cause (PINCH ME)**, stop culprit drugs, **multicomponent care** (reorientation, glasses, hearing aids, sleep, mobility, fluids - prevents a third); dementia: disclose, **cognitive stimulation**, exercise, carer support; **donepezil 5 mg at night x 4-6 weeks -> 10 mg** for mild-moderate AD, DLB, PDD.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Memantine 5 mg -> +5 mg weekly -> 20 mg OD** for moderate-severe AD or added to donepezil (10 mg if CrCl 5-29); rivastigmine patch 4.6 -> 9.5 mg/24 h; galantamine PR 8 -> 16-24 mg; **no cognitive drug in vascular or frontotemporal dementia** (SSRI/trazodone for FTD behaviour).`],[`Treatment - antipsychotic (danger only)`,`Antipsychotic only for danger or severe distress: **haloperidol 0.25-0.5 mg, max 2-3 mg/24 h** in elderly; **quetiapine 12.5-25 mg in Lewy body/Parkinson** (never haloperidol/risperidone); BPSD: **risperidone 0.25 mg BD up to 1 mg BD for up to 6 weeks**, review 6-12 weekly; benzodiazepine only in withdrawal delirium.`],[`Special groups`,`**Alcohol withdrawal delirium**: lorazepam/diazepam + **thiamine before glucose**; **Lewy body/PD**: severe neuroleptic sensitivity; **renal**: memantine 10 mg; heart block/bradycardia: avoid cholinesterase inhibitors; young onset (under 65): specialist work-up.`],[`Complications`,`Delirium: falls, pressure sores, longer stay, institutionalisation, **death**, later dementia; dementia: BPSD, wandering, malnutrition, aspiration, elder abuse; drugs: **antipsychotics raise stroke (3-fold) and death (1.6-fold)**, cholinesterase inhibitor bradycardia and syncope.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Any sudden change in dementia = delirium until proved otherwise**; fever + neck stiffness, focal signs, fall on anticoagulant (subdural), **rapid progression over weeks**, onset under 65, early gait + incontinence (NPH), bradycardia under 50 on donepezil; refer DLB, FTD, refractory BPSD.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Reassess cognition 4-8 weeks after delirium** (unmasks dementia), stop any antipsychotic started; dementia review **every 6 months**; cholinesterase inhibitor - pulse and GI effects at 4-6 weeks, benefit at 3-6 months, **continue while benefit**; advance care planning.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Lancet 2024: control **BP, diabetes, LDL, smoking, alcohol, inactivity, hearing loss (hearing aids), vision loss, depression, isolation, air pollution**, education; deprescribe via **Beers 2023**; ARDSI, DMHP, RPwD certification, MHCA advance directive.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Anti-amyloid antibodies (lecanemab, donanemab)**: NICE final draft 2025 did not recommend NHS use (modest 4-6 month benefit, ARIA, cost; reconsideration after 2026 appeals) - specialist only, not primary care; NICE NG97 and CG103 otherwise unchanged.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Three questions - **how fast, is attention impaired, is consciousness altered**: delirium is yes, yes, yes; antipsychotics only sedate delirium; **never haloperidol in Lewy body dementia**; cholinesterase inhibitors are vagotonic - check pulse; 'I don't know' answers = pseudodementia.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute confusion in an older patient`,steps:[{label:`ABC, capillary glucose, SpO2`,detail:`Hypoglycaemia: dextrose - thiamine first in drinkers`,tone:`warn`},{label:`4AT 4 or more / CAM positive?`,detail:`Inattention + acute fluctuating change = delirium`,tone:`decision`},{label:`PINCH ME search`,detail:`Pain, Infection, Nutrition, Constipation, Hydration, Medication, Environment`},{label:`Bloods, urine, ECG, CXR; bladder scan and PR`,detail:`CT if fall, focal signs or anticoagulant; LP if meningism`},{label:`Multicomponent non-drug care`,detail:`Reorient, glasses, hearing aids, family, sleep, mobilise`},{label:`Haloperidol 0.25-0.5 mg only if dangerous distress`,detail:`Quetiapine in Lewy/PD; benzodiazepine only in withdrawal`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Recovery; recheck cognition at 4-8 weeks`,detail:`Stop antipsychotic; record the episode`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Dementia drug ladder`,steps:[{label:`Non-drug care first`,detail:`Cognitive stimulation, exercise, carer support, treat BPSD triggers`},{label:`Mild-moderate AD, DLB, PDD: cholinesterase inhibitor`,detail:`Donepezil 5 mg at night x 4-6 weeks then 10 mg; check pulse`},{label:`Moderate-severe AD: add or switch to memantine`,detail:`5 mg, up 5 mg weekly to 20 mg OD (10 mg if CrCl 5-29)`},{label:`Severe BPSD with risk: short antipsychotic`,detail:`Risperidone 0.25-1 mg BD up to 6 weeks; never in Lewy body`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Anti-amyloid antibodies - specialist only`,detail:`Lecanemab/donanemab: ARIA, MRI monitoring; not NICE-recommended (2025)`,tone:`decision`}]}]}};export{e as default};